and you saw how you can get cross-sections. You’ll get a cross-section of a defect,
如何获取截面。你将得到一个缺陷的截面图，

29
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:53,040
and from that, you’ll be able to say, “Oh, that defect is between these layers, so it must have come from this section of the flow. Oh, we have inline metrology in that section,
从这个截面中，你就能判断：“哦，这个缺陷位于这两层之间，所以它一定来自流程中的这个环节。而这个环节我们已经有在线量测了。”

30
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:57,200
which has identified defects. Those are the defects we need to go after.”
已经发现了缺陷，这些就是我们需要解决的缺陷。

31
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:05,920
What we’ve learned is that you don’t want to do too much innovation in one node, because the more innovation that you do, the more complex it becomes.
我们学到的是，不要在一个节点上做过多的创新，因为创新越多，系统就会变得越复杂。

32
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:14,480
While you’ll be able to get that to work, you need to hit a certain schedule, and that’s the schedule that your customer ultimately wants. Having that predictable schedule is a key piece.
虽然你能让这事运作起来，但你必须达到某个时间表，而这正是客户最终希望的时间表。拥有可预测的时间表是关键。

33
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:24,240
You’ve got to figure out how much innovation you can put in in order to meet the customer’s requirements in the timeline that they need it. Intel learned how costly that tradeoff can
你必须弄清楚，在客户要求的时间节点内，能投入多少创新来满足他们的需求。英特尔在10纳米制程节点上深刻体会到了这种权衡的代价，

34
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:28,880
be with delays to its 10 nanometer node, whose original target launch date was 2016,
其最初的目标是2016年发布，

35
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:34,880
but which didn’t begin manufacturing until 2018. Usually Intel has been the keeper of Moore’s Law,
但实际量产直到2018年才开始。通常来说，英特尔一直是摩尔定律的守护者。

36
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:44,320
a new cadence of manufacturing every two years, but their last set of products slipped almost four years. Intel today having its worst day in four months,
每两年一次的制造节奏，但上一轮产品发布却推迟了将近四年。英特尔今天遭遇了四个月以来最糟糕的一天，股价已下跌超过15%。

37
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:57,200
the stock now down more than 15%. I also want to point out Taiwan Semiconductor up 13% off of Intel’s bad news. Walk me through what lessons you think Intel
我还想指出，台积电在英特尔坏消息的背景下上涨了13%。请跟我梳理一下，你认为英特尔从10纳米制程中吸

38
00:03:57,200 --> 00:04:06,560
has learned from 10 nanometer. Ten nanometer was a good example of where we had very aggressive targets. We were doing what was called hyperscaling
取了哪些教训？10纳米是一个非常典型的例子，当时我们的目标极为激进，我们当时所做的被称为超大规模扩展。

39
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:10,640
at the time, so we were scaling much faster than what had been historically done.
当时，我们的扩展速度远超以往的历史水平。

40
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:23,040
In order to do that, we came up with all sorts of innovative ideas on how we could achieve that without new tools, because the tools weren’t ready.
为了实现这一点，我们想出了各种创新的点子，探索如何在没有新工具的情况下达成目标，因为那些工具当时还准备就绪。

41
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:26,560
That put a lot of burden on the process and a lot of burden on the engineers.
这给工艺带来了很大负担，也给工程师带来了很大压力。

42
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:30,480
Now, at the end of the day, 10 nanometer is a great technology.
说到底，10纳米是一项非常出色的技术。

43
00:04:30,480 --> 00:04:38,240
It has world-class yield, it has world-class performance, it has world-class cost, but it took us longer to do that than what we would have liked.
它具备世界级的良率、世界级的性能和世界级的成本，但实现这一目标所花的时间比我们预期的要长。

44
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:46,800
So we’ve tried to take that learning into future technology nodes and say, “Hey, we need to limit the amount of innovation we put in there to make sure that we keep it on schedule.”
所以我们试图将这一经验应用到未来的技术节点中，提醒自己：“嘿，我们需要控制创新的投入量，以确保项目按时推进。”

45
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:52,160
What do you think was the main technology that should’ve been saved for a future version?
你觉得应该留到未来版本的主技术是什么？

46
00:04:52,160 --> 00:05:03,200
EUV was probably the big thing. When we started to define 10 nanometer, the conclusion was EUV was not ready. EUV is extreme ultraviolet lithography.
极紫外光刻可能是最重要的突破。当我们开始定义10纳米制程时，结论是极紫外光刻技术还不成熟。极紫外光刻就是极端紫外光刻。

47
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:13,440
It prints chip patterns with light at a wavelength of 13.5 nanometers, about 14 times shorter than that emitted by previous tools that chipmakers had relied on.
它使用波长为13.5纳米的光来打印芯片图案，波长仅为之前芯片制造商所依赖工具发出光的约十四分之一。

48
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:18,000
The shorter the wavelength, the finer the features that a single exposure can print.
波长越短，单次曝光所能印刷的特征就越精细。

49
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,760
But Intel decided this technology wasn’t going to be ready in time.
但英特尔决定，这项技术无法按时完成。

50
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:24,960
TSMC transitioned to EUV much sooner.
台积电更早地转向了 EUV。

51
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:32,480
It put EUV into volume manufacturing in 2019 and ramped it up further on its 5nm node in 2020.
它于2019年将极紫外光刻技术投入量产，并在2020年其5纳米制程节点上进一步扩大了应用。

52
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:43,920
Intel’s first EUV process did not reach products until 2023, so TSMC had about four more years of experience with these machines. Yesterday, we were at the research
英特尔的首款 EUV 制程直到 2023 年才应用于产品，因此台积电在这些设备上的经验多了大约四年。昨天，

53
00:05:43,920 --> 00:05:50,880
lab getting a preview of 2D materials and CFET and ruthenium interconnects.
我们在研究实验室提前看到了二维材料、CFET 以及钌互连的进展。

54
00:05:50,880 --> 00:05:55,520
I’m curious about how you decide whether that technology is mature enough for the next node.
我很好奇你是如何判断这项技术是否已经成熟到可以用于下一个制程节点的。

55
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:00,640
One thing that did also come out of 10 nanometer is we started to define a set of maturity metrics.
10纳米制程的一个成果，就是我们开始定义了一套成熟度指标。

56
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:10,000
We use those maturity metrics in a process which we call phase gate, where we would say, “To go from pathfinding into development, these are the criteria that we need to go hit.”
我们使用这些成熟度指标，通过一个称为“阶段门”的流程，来判断“从探索阶段进入开发阶段，我们需要达到哪些标准”。

57
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:18,000
There are yield criteria, defect criteria, performance criteria, and we need to hit those in order to move to the next set of the flow. If there’s something that doesn’t make that,
有良率标准、缺陷标准和性能标准，我们必须达到这些标准才能进入流程的下一步。如果某项指标未达标，

58
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,200
then it misses that, and it goes on to the next technology.
就会被剔除，然后进入下一项技术。

59
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:31,200
We focus on the items that are ready and we can run and put into a process, because one of the key things is staying on schedule. Staying on schedule is hard partly because new
我们专注于那些已经准备就绪、可以投入流程运行的项目，因为保持进度准时是关键。保持进度准时之所以困难，部分原因在于新型晶

60
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:41,280
transistor designs take decades to mature. Modern chip transistors evolved from the planar MOS transistor, demonstrated in 1960. The history of transistors since has been
体管设计需要数十年才能成熟。现代芯片晶体管起源于1960年展示的平面MOS晶体管，此后晶体管的发展历程就是从二维

61
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:50,400
the progression from two dimensions into three. Each development that Chris goes on to describe, from FinFET to gate-all-around, is a step in that progression.
向三维演进的过程。克里斯接下来所描述的每一项技术进步，从FinFET到环绕栅极，都是这一演进过程中的一步。

62
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:55,520
So it’ll help to first look at a basic transistor and how its parts have changed over time.
因此，首先了解一个基本晶体管及其各部分随时间的变化会很有帮助。

63
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:05,360
A transistor is basically a switch. When it’s turned on, it allows current to flow between its two ends, which are called the source and the drain.
晶体管本质上就是一个开关。当它打开时，电流就能在其两个端点之间流动，这两个端点分别称为源极和漏极。

64
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:11,280
A piece called a gate sits on top of the silicon between the source and the drain.
一个称为栅极的部件位于硅片之上，介于源极和漏极之间。

65
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:22,080
Applying a positive voltage to the gate turns the switch on, because the positive voltage pulls negatively charged electrons in the silicon beneath up toward the gate.
向栅极施加正电压会使开关导通，因为正电压会将硅层中带负电的电子吸引到栅极下方。

66
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:25,920
However, a thin layer of insulation stops them from actually reaching the gate.
然而，一层薄薄的绝缘层阻止它们真正接触到栅极。

67
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:36,000
So the electrons collect just beneath that insulation and form a thin conducting path from the source to the drain. This is called the channel.
因此，电子会聚集在绝缘层下方，形成一条从源极到漏极的细导电通道。这被称为沟道。

68
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,280
Remove the voltage and the electrons disperse, the channel disappears, and the switch turns off.
去掉电压后，电子就会散开，沟道消失，开关也就关闭了。

69
00:07:41,280 --> 00:07:50,240
As transistors shrank, the source and the drain got so close together that current could flow through the channel even when the switch was supposed to be off.
随着晶体管尺寸缩小，源极和漏极之间的距离变得如此之近，以至于即使开关本应处于关闭状态，电流仍能通过沟道流动。

70
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:54,960
We needed a better way to control the channel and prevent current from leaking through.
我们需要一种更好的方法来控制通道，防止电流泄漏。

71
00:07:54,960 --> 00:08:00,320
The fix was to give the gate a tighter grip on the channel by touching it from more sides.
解决方法是通过从更多侧面接触沟道，让栅极对沟道的夹持更紧。

72
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:04,880
In the original planar transistor, the gate only touches the channel from the top.
在原始的平面晶体管中，栅极仅从顶部接触沟道。

73
00:08:04,880 --> 00:08:09,520
The FinFET transistor squeezes the channel into a thin vertical fin of silicon.
鳍式晶体管将沟道挤压成一根细长的垂直硅鳍。

74
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:14,480
The gate drapes over the fin, so it controls the channel from three sides.
栅极覆盖在鳍片上，从而从三个方向控制沟道。

75
00:08:14,480 --> 00:08:22,000
The next progression from there was to cover not only the top and the sides of the channel, but also the bottom. This technology is called gate-all-around, and
接下来的进展是，不仅包裹通道的顶部和侧面，还覆盖了底部。这种技术被称为环绕栅极，

76
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:27,440
it slices the channel into thin ribbons, each of which gets completely wrapped around by the gate.
它将通道切割成细长的条带，每一条都由栅极完全包裹。

77
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:31,760
But getting gate-all-around to work required new materials and new manufacturing steps.
但要让环绕栅极技术发挥作用，需要采用新材料和新的制造工艺。

78
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:42,720
To build these floating ribbons of silicon, you first sandwich them between alternating layers of a temporary placeholder material, which holds the space where the gate will eventually go.
要制造这些悬浮的硅条，首先要将它们夹在交替的临时填充材料层之间，这些材料会占据未来栅极所在的空间。

79
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:52,000
We use a placeholder because the materials in the real gate wouldn’t survive the processing that comes after, which involves baking the wafer at around 1,000 degrees Celsius.
我们使用占位符，因为实际栅极材料无法经受后续工艺的高温处理，该工艺需要将晶圆烘烤至约1000摄氏度。

80
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:59,600
Once these hot steps are done, chipmakers etch away the placeholder, leaving the ribbons suspended. Then they fill the gaps around every ribbon
完成这些高温工艺步骤后，芯片制造商蚀刻掉临时结构，使栅极条带悬空。接着在每根条带周围填充

81
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:09,040
with a thin coat of a special insulator called a high-k material, whose purpose is to let the gate pull more strongly on the channel. And then on top of this material,
一层薄薄的特殊绝缘材料，称为高k材料，其作用是让栅极能更有力地控制沟道。然后在该材料之上，再铺设栅极本身的金属。

82
00:09:09,040 --> 00:09:20,880
they put the metal of the gate itself. FinFET was demonstrated in a lab in 1989 and reached volume production at Intel in 2011. A gate-all-around transistor had been demonstrated
FinFET于1989年在实验室中首次展示，2011年在英特尔实现量产。环绕栅极晶体管也已成功演示

83
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:27,520
a year earlier, in 1988, but Samsung didn’t begin producing gate-all-around chips until 2022.
早在1988年，但三星直到2022年才开始生产环绕栅极芯片。

84
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:32,000
So these ideas, FinFET and gate-all-around, were developed around the same time,
这些技术，FinFET 和环绕栅极，

85
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:41,280
but they reached production a decade apart. I wanted to ask Chris why it took so long to get to this current gate-all-around technology. Maybe you can walk me through the process of
虽然几乎在同一时期开发出来，但进入量产却相隔了十年。我想问问克里斯，为什么这种环绕栅极技术的实现花了这么长时间？你能带我了解一下进

86
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:44,640
getting into high-volume manufacturing. I want to understand concretely,
入大规模制造的过程吗？我想具体地弄清楚这一点。

87
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:56,240
once you have an idea that, “Hey, we’re going to make the channel smaller, we’re going to surround it…” All the other problems of material science, figuring out how to not destroy the gate,
一旦有了这样的想法：“嘿，我们要把通道变窄，把它包围起来……” 那么材料科学中的其他问题，比如如何避免损坏栅极，

88
00:09:56,240 --> 00:09:58,800
how do they actually get figured out? Why does it take so long?
这些问题到底是怎么解决的？为什么需要这么长时间？

89
00:09:58,800 --> 00:10:02,080
Gate-all-around’s a good example, because back in the ’90s we knew of this idea.
环绕栅极是一个很好的例子，因为在上世纪90年代，我们就已经了解过这个概念。

90
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:08,800
But we knew there were going to be these problems on how you put this into high-volume manufacturing. At the same time, there are other
但我们知道，要把这项技术投入大规模制造，必然会遇到这些问题。与此同时，还有其他技术，

91
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:13,520
things like high-K metal gate. There’s strained silicon,
比如高介电常数金属栅极、应变硅、FinFET等已经存在。

92
00:10:13,520 --> 00:10:24,880
there’s FinFETs that are out there. It’s a question of when’s the right time to go and tackle those? Well, you don’t want to tackle something before you have to. If you have these other things that can drive
关键在于，什么时候才是攻克这些技术的合适时机？你当然不想在还没到必须解决的时候就提前去处理。如果还有其他技术可以推动进展，那就先专注于那些。

93
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:27,600
performance, you would go and do that. So we went through this path.
性能方面，你就会去这么做。所以我们走过了这条路径。

94
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:35,440
We did strained silicon, high-K metal gate, we did FinFETs, and then it became, “Well, we need to do things further. How do we get there?”
我们做过应变硅、高K金属栅极，也做过鳍式场效应晶体管，后来就变成“嗯，我们得继续往前走，该怎么做到呢？”

95
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:38,880
Well, gate-all-around’s really the ultimate transistor out there.
嗯，环绕栅极晶体管才是目前最顶尖的晶体管技术。

96
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:43,440
Maybe it’s time to go and start tackling some of those issues that you have.
也许该去着手解决你的一些问题了。

97
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:48,000
You’re right, now you have to look at how do you put high-K metal gate in with gate-all-around.
你说得对，现在你得考虑如何将高K金属栅极与环绕栅极结合起来。

98
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:52,320
It’s that innovation that takes time. Sometimes you need new materials.
正是这种创新需要时间，有时你需要新的材料。

99
00:10:52,320 --> 00:11:01,760
Sometimes you need a different tool in order to be able to do some of the things that you need to go do, and that requires invention from the tool vendors.
有时候，你需要不同的工具才能完成一些必须做的事，这就要求工具供应商进行创新。

100
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:04,960
You have to go to the tool vendor and say, “Hey, I need a tool that can do XYZ.
你得去找设备供应商说：“嘿，我需要一台能做 XYZ 的设备。”

101
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:09,600
I need a gas that can deposit this particular material that goes into that.
我需要一种气体，能将这种特定材料沉积到那个位置。

102
00:11:09,600 --> 00:11:14,240
I need a heating chuck that has this characteristic to it.”
我需要一个具备这种特性的加热夹具。

103
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:21,440
All those things take time. Some of it’s iterative, where you have to run a gate-all-around transistor and say, “Oh, well, that didn’t work.
所有这些事情都需要时间，有些是反复迭代的，你得运行一个环绕栅极晶体管，然后发现：“哦，不行，这个没成功。”

104
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:34,320
Let me figure out what was wrong with my hypothesis and try it again with some different structure.” How has the nature of R&D changed from the era of
让我弄清楚我假设中哪里出了问题，然后换一种不同的结构再试一次。从过去单纯把平面晶体管越做越小、

105
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:39,440
just making planar transistors smaller and smaller and getting the benefits of Dennard scaling,
享受丹纳德缩放效应的时代，到今天这个全新的世界，研发的本质究竟发生

106
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:43,840
to the world we’re in today? Is it just a totally different kind of R&D process? Previously it was,
了怎样的变化？这难道真的是一种完全不同的研发模式吗？以前并非如此。

107
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:52,320
“Let’s make things smaller, smaller, smaller.” Now you look at all these other facets of performance at the same time, and improvements in size don’t come as easily.
“让我们把东西做得越来越小，越来越小，越来越小。”但现在你还要同时关注性能的其他各个方面，尺寸的进一步缩小就没那么容易了。

108
00:11:52,320 --> 00:11:55,520
Walk me through whether there was some crucial change in the R&D process at that point.
请跟我梳理一下，当时研发流程是否发生了什么关键性变化。

109
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:59,600
If you look at the ’80s and ’90s, yes, it was driven really by Dennard scaling, lithography.
如果你看看80年代和90年代，确实主要由登纳德缩放定律和光刻技术推动。

110
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:03,840
We just made things smaller, transistors got faster, they got more reliable,
我们只是让东西变得更小了，晶体管变得更快、更可靠，

111
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:11,920
and it was a very predictable cadence. When we started to hit the 2000s, we saw, “That’s not good enough anymore. We need to do something different.”
节奏非常可预测。但进入2000年代后，我们意识到，“这已经不够了，我们必须做点不一样的事。”

112
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:21,040
To me, it became this golden age of innovation. For each technology node, how are you going to provide more scaling? How are you going to provide more performance?
对我来说，这成了一个创新的黄金时代。每个技术节点，你该如何实现更大的扩展性？又该如何提供更高的性能？

113
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:24,942
It drove things like strained silicon. It drove high-K metal gate.
它推动了应变硅技术的发展，也推动了高K金属栅极技术的发展。

114
00:12:24,942 --> 00:12:29,040
It drove FinFETs. It drove gate-all-around. It drove backside power.
它推动了鳍式晶体管的发展，推动了环绕栅极技术的演进，也推动了背面供电的实现。

115
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:35,920
These are things that in the ’80s and ’90s you would be like, “Oh no, we’re not touching any of that,” because we just continued to have the same process flow.
这些东西在80年代和90年代的时候，你会说：“天哪，我们可碰都不碰”，因为那时我们一直沿用着同样的工艺流程。

116
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:46,080
We just scaled it, scaled it, scaled it. But for somebody like me who’s a transistor junkie, it’s awesome, because now the playbook’s open.
我们刚刚完成了规模的扩展，不断扩展，持续扩展。但对于像我这样痴迷于晶体管的人来说，这太棒了，因为现在整个方案已经完全公开了。

117
00:12:46,080 --> 00:12:49,760
You can do all sorts of things out there. You can bring in new materials.
你可以在那里做各种各样的事情，比如引入新材料。

118
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,960
Just look at the number of materials we used to use in the ’90s versus the materials today.
看看我们上世纪90年代使用的材料数量，再对比一下今天使用的材料数量。

119
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:56,480
It’s probably gone up 10X. There’s a lot of innovation.
可能已经提升了10倍。创新非常多。

120
00:12:56,480 --> 00:13:01,280
There’s atomic layer deposition. You’re controlling this stuff atom by atom.
有原子层沉积。你是在逐个原子地控制这些材料。

121
00:13:02,160 --> 00:13:05,200
So it’s changed a lot how you approach the process.
所以你处理这个过程的方式已经改变了很多。

122
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:16,080
Before, it was, “Try to not change anything.” Now it’s, “Take the sledgehammer and start tearing the thing up and come up with some new idea.” For 14A, Intel is using a new kind
以前是“尽量不要改变任何东西”，现在则是“拿起大锤，把旧东西彻底拆掉，想出全新的点子”。对于14A制程，英特尔正在使用一种新型光刻机：

123
00:13:16,080 --> 00:13:29,280
of lithography machine: High-NA EUV. It uses the same extreme ultraviolet light as today’s EUV machines, but changes the optics. NA, or numerical aperture, is a measure of how
高数值孔径极紫外光刻机。它和当前的极紫外光刻机一样，使用相同的极紫外光，但改变了光学系统。NA，即数值孔径，

124
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:34,080
wide a cone of light the machine’s mirrors can focus onto the wafer.
衡量的是光刻机镜片能聚焦到晶圆上的光锥宽度。

125
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:38,880
The wider the cone you can focus, the smaller the spot that your light can carve.
你能聚焦的光锥越宽，光线能刻画出的光斑就越小。

126
00:13:38,880 --> 00:13:45,360
Standard EUV machines have an NA of 0.33. High-NA widens it to 0.55.
标准的 EUV 光刻机数值孔径为 0.33，高数值孔径则将其提升至 0.55。

127
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:51,040
So High-NA can print features about 1.7 times smaller in a single pass.
因此，高数值孔径光刻技术可在一次曝光中实现约1.7倍更小的特征尺寸。

128
00:13:51,040 --> 00:14:00,800
When features are smaller than the lithography machine can print, chipmakers have to split the pattern across several passes, each printing a different set of features.
当特征尺寸小于光刻机的打印能力时，芯片制造商就必须将图案分成多次曝光，每次曝光打印一组不同的特征。

129
00:14:00,800 --> 00:14:06,000
The most tightly packed patterns can require two, three, or even four passes.
最密集的图案可能需要两次、三次，甚至四次曝光。

130
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:10,960
Each additional pass takes up valuable time on a machine that costs around $200 million,
每次额外的加工都会占用价值约2亿美元的机器的宝贵时间，

131
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:19,200
and it introduces another opportunity for defects. High-NA machines cost about twice as much, but they can reduce the number of passes that are needed.
同时增加出现缺陷的机会。高数值孔径机器的成本约为其两倍，但能显著减少所需的加工次数。

132
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:27,200
So the question which determines whether they justify their higher price is how much more time they save and how much they reduce the risk of defects.
决定它们是否物有所值的关键问题是，它们能节省多少时间，以及能将缺陷风险降低多少。

133
00:14:27,200 --> 00:14:39,840
Now you guys are using High-NA EUV tools for 14A, whereas TSMC has said that they don’t make economic sense yet for them. How do you decide whether the next litho tool is
现在你们在14A制程上已经开始使用高数值孔径极紫外光刻工具，而台积电表示，对他们而言这些工具目前还缺乏经济意义。

134
00:14:39,840 --> 00:14:45,200
the one that is ready to adopt or not? High-NA’s a good example of where,
你们如何判断下一台光刻机是否已经具备采用条件？高数值孔径就是一个很好的例子，

135
00:14:45,200 --> 00:14:56,160
because of the architecture of our technology versus TSMC’s, we make different conclusions off of the same tool set. We use backside power.
由于我们的技术架构与台积电不同，面对同一套工具，得出了不同的结论。我们采用了背面供电技术。

136
00:14:56,160 --> 00:15:01,680
That means that you actually alleviate some of the pitch requirements you have on the front side.
这意味着你实际上减轻了正面的间距要求。

137
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:04,800
That allows you to print that directly with High-NA.
这让你可以直接用高数值孔径光刻技术进行打印。

138
00:15:04,800 --> 00:15:12,480
TSMC has to run tighter pitches because they don’t have backside power, and they can’t do that with High-NA, so they have to use double patterning EUV.
台积电必须采用更紧密的间距，因为他们无法实现背面供电，而且在高数值孔径光刻下也无法做到这一点，因此他们必须采用双重曝光 EUV 工艺。

139
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:16,560
So for them, it’s not very helpful. For us, it is very helpful.
对他们来说，这没什么帮助。对我们来说，却非常有用。

140
00:15:16,560 --> 00:15:24,880
Now at 14A, we’ve left it as an option, and it’s something that we’ll have a discussion with the customer on, because there’s some value proposition to it, and there’s some
现在在14A制程上，我们把它作为一个可选项，具体是否采用，我们会与客户进行讨论，因为这项技术有其价值主张，

141
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:31,280
complexity that it adds. Chris is referring to a feature called backside power. A chip has two different kinds of wiring.
同时也带来一定复杂性。克里斯提到的是名为背面供电的功能。芯片有两种不同的布线方式。

142
00:15:31,280 --> 00:15:35,440
Power lines deliver electricity to the transistors, and they need to be thick.
电源线将电力输送至晶体管，它们必须足够粗。

143
00:15:35,440 --> 00:15:38,320
Signal lines let the transistors talk to each other.
信号线让晶体管之间能够相互通信。

144
00:15:38,320 --> 00:15:46,560
They’re much thinner, but they need to be spaced apart so that the signal from one wire doesn’t interfere with the signal from another. In the past, all of these power lines
它们要薄得多，但必须保持间距，以免一根导线的信号干扰到另一根导线。过去，

145
00:15:46,560 --> 00:15:50,160
and signal lines had to be crammed together on top of the transistors.
所有的电源线和信号线都必须挤在晶体管上方。

146
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:55,760
Intel’s newest process moves these power lines below the transistors, to the back of the wafer.
英特尔最新的制程将这些电源线路移至晶体管下方，位于晶圆背面。

147
00:15:55,760 --> 00:16:00,160
This frees up space on top, so the signal lines don’t have to be packed as tightly.
这腾出了上方空间，因此信号线无需挤得那么紧密。

148
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:09,440
This change makes High-NA worth it for Intel. Without backside power, the wiring is so tight that it would require multiple passes to print, even with High-NA.
这一改变让英特尔采用高数值孔径光刻成为值得的抉择。若没有背面供电，布线将过于密集，即使使用高数值孔径，也需多次曝光才能完成。

149
00:16:09,440 --> 00:16:13,280
With backside power, a single High-NA pass is enough.
采用背面供电后，仅需一次高数值孔径光刻即可完成。

150
00:16:13,280 --> 00:16:17,680
Outside companies can now commission Intel to manufacture chips that they’ve designed.
现在，外部公司可以委托英特尔代工生产他们设计的芯片。

151
00:16:17,680 --> 00:16:24,000
They follow Intel’s process design kit, or PDK: the menu of what a given process can build,
它们遵循英特尔的工艺设计套件（PDK）：即特定工艺能够实现的结构清单及其构建规则。

152
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:30,640
and the rules for building it. Do you need to lock that into your PDK right now for customers who are planning on developing on 14A?
对于那些计划在14A工艺上开发的客户，你现在是否需要将这些内容锁定到你的PDK中？

153
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:39,200
It’s an option in the PDK today, and we’re handling it as a customer-by-customer discussion as to whether or not they want to go down that path or not.
目前PDK中已提供这一选项，至于客户是否选择走这条路，我们正根据每个客户的具体情况分别进行沟通。

154
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:44,000
For 18A, there was public reporting that there were delays in or issues with the PDK,
对于18A制程，有公开报道指出PDK存在延迟或问题，

155
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:51,280
such that some instructions didn’t work, and it was difficult for people to use your foundry to build their chips. Tell me about the process of Intel
导致部分指令无法使用，客户很难通过你们的晶圆厂来制造芯片。请谈谈英特尔如何转型成为晶圆代工厂，

156
00:16:51,280 --> 00:17:00,160
becoming a foundry and then having a PDK that other customers can use to make whatever chip they’re making as well. There’s definitely a learning curve to
并开发出可供其他客户使用的PDK，让他们也能顺利制造自己的芯片。这其中确实存在一个学习曲线。

157
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:05,920
how you become a foundry. That’s something that our competitors have gone through as well. PDK is a good example of that.
你如何成为一家代工厂，这正是我们的竞争对手也经历过的。PDK 就是其中的一个好例子。

158
00:17:05,920 --> 00:17:12,800
For decades, we’ve been an IDM. We’ve gone to our product teams and shown them our transistor data, and we share that data back and forth, and we haven’t
几十年来，我们一直是一家IDM。我们曾向产品团队展示我们的晶体管数据，并在双方之间来回共享这些数据，

159
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:22,400
had a formal PDK-type process. So that’s a new process that we’ve had to implement on 18A. 18A started out not having a PDK.
但从未有过正式的PDK流程。因此，这是我们为18A制程新建立的流程。18A最初并没有PDK。

160
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:33,280
There’s definitely a lot of feedback we’ve gotten from the customers as to what they expect for quality and maturity of the PDK and what sort of information they want to see in there.
我们确实收到了大量客户反馈，关于他们对PDK的质量和成熟度的期望，以及他们希望在其中看到哪些信息。

161
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:40,160
We’ve tried to incorporate that into making sure that we have a PDK that meets our customers’ requirements. That’s where we’ve ended up.
我们已努力将这一点融入其中，确保我们提供的PDK能满足客户的需求。这就是我们最终的成果。

162
00:17:40,720 --> 00:17:45,600
We’re starting to get PDKs that have this, and 14A will have that from the get-go.
我们已经开始获得具备这一特性的PDK，而14A从一开始就将支持该功能。

163
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:56,240
It has very high quality PDKs. Again, there are maturity metrics for what is a PDK 0.5 versus a 0.9 versus a 1.0. We’re constantly going through and taking
它的PDK质量非常高。再次强调，PDK的成熟度有明确的衡量标准，比如0.5版、0.9版和1.0版之间的区别。

164
00:17:56,240 --> 00:18:00,880
customer feedback and incorporating that into the quality of our PDK.
我们一直在持续收集客户反馈，并将其融入到PDK的质量提升中。

165
00:18:00,880 --> 00:18:04,960
For most of its history, Intel’s fabs made only Intel’s chips.
在大部分历史中，英特尔的晶圆厂只生产英特尔的芯片。

166
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:07,680
That could be part of what allowed TSMC to gain an edge.
这可能是台积电能够取得优势的部分原因。

167
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:12,880
Serving many customers gave TSMC more wafers to learn from, more revenue to reinvest,
为众多客户提供服务让台积电获得了更多晶圆用于学习，更多收入用于再投资，

168
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:20,400
and more pressure to hit deadlines. Basically, all enterprise customers have a shared set of product requirements that you have to meet if you want to even
也面临更大的交付期限压力。基本上，所有企业客户都有一套共同的产品需求，

169
00:18:20,400 --> 00:18:29,840
