00:27:31,920 --> 00:27:41,280
Jane Street use magic-trace every single day. If you want to work somewhere where you’re trusted to pursue the ideas that you think matter most, Jane Street is hiring researchers and engineers.
人员每天都使用 magic-trace。如果你希望在一个信任你去追求心中最重要的想法的环境中工作，Jane Street 正在招聘研究员和工程师。

256
00:27:41,280 --> 00:27:53,200
Go to janestreet.com/dwarkesh to learn more. One of the pieces that you observed this morning was a special clean that we were looking at, which would eliminate the nodule.
访问 janestreet.com/dwarkesh 了解更多信息。你们今天上午看到的其中一项内容，是我们正在研究的一项特殊清洁工艺，可消除结节。

257
00:27:53,200 --> 00:27:56,160
The nodule would deposit, and then we’d get rid of it.
结节会沉积下来，然后我们就把它清除掉。

258
00:27:56,160 --> 00:27:59,520
Some of the other discussion was about the source of the nodule.
其他讨论还涉及结节的来源。

259
00:27:59,520 --> 00:28:07,680
Get rid of the source of the nodule. There’s some issue. How do you decide what the source of it is? For the most part, we work highly parallel.
把结节的根源去掉。这里有些问题，你如何判断它的根源是什么？大部分情况下，我们采用高度并行的方式工作。

260
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:15,680
How do you decide? It’s not always evident what the easiest way to fix the problem is, so you work on all the potential fixes.
你怎么决定？问题的最简单解决方法并不总是显而易见，所以你要尝试所有可能的修复方案。

261
00:28:16,480 --> 00:28:27,440
Sometimes it’s not the one you expect first. Once we’ve identified it as a primary problem for the technology, that’s where this Hot Topics meeting gets involved.
有时候，最先遇到的并不是你预期的那个问题。一旦我们确认这是该技术的主要问题，这个热点话题会议就会介入。

262
00:28:27,440 --> 00:28:36,320
That’s how we align the resources into those engineering teams so that we can accelerate the work. You said that there’s some
这就是我们如何将资源调配到工程团队，以加速工作进度。你说过，

263
00:28:36,320 --> 00:28:40,640
fast-track process where wafers can move through faster if they’re bottlenecking some experiment.
如果晶圆在某个实验中成为瓶颈，可以通过快速通道流程更快地流转。

264
00:28:40,640 --> 00:28:44,240
We have a tiered system of what we call hot boxes in the factory.
我们在工厂里有一个所谓的热箱分级系统。

265
00:28:44,960 --> 00:28:54,880
Every grade of hot box goes a little bit faster. It’s just how the factory prioritizes that particular lot, that block of silicon that’s running through the factory.
每个等级的热盒运行速度都会稍快一些，这取决于工厂对特定批次、正在流水线上运行的那块硅片的优先级安排。

266
00:28:55,680 --> 00:29:00,480
Our highest grade of hot box, HotBox 1, races through the factory.
我们最高级别的热箱，HotBox 1，在工厂中飞速运转。

267
00:29:00,480 --> 00:29:04,800
It gets priority at every single tool, but it’s a drag on the entire factory.
它在每个工具上都享有优先权，但却拖累了整个工厂的效率。

268
00:29:04,800 --> 00:29:16,000
If you had a factory person in here, they don’t like playing that card, because you end up using the tools inefficiently so that you can go super fast.
如果你这儿有个工厂人员，他们可不乐意出这张牌，因为那样会导致工具使用效率低下，最终你虽然跑得飞快，却浪费了资源。

269
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:23,440
Think of it as a highway, and you set aside that one lane for the carpool, which everyone could use, but you’re saying, “Okay, it’s just the carpool.”
把它想象成一条高速公路，你专门划出一条车道给拼车使用，大家都能用，但你强调说：“好，这条车道只供拼车用。”

270
00:29:23,440 --> 00:29:30,720
That’s our HotBox 1. Everyone clear out of that lane except for that one experiment, and that one experiment races down the highway.
这就是我们的热盒1号。除了那一个实验，其他人都离开那个车道，就让那个实验冲上高速公路。

271
00:29:30,720 --> 00:29:41,280
Kevin’s job is deciding what goes onto the production line now, but the day before, I went to Intel’s research lab with Jack Kavalieros to see ideas that aren’t ready for the node yet.
凯文的工作是决定现在哪些产品能上生产线，但前一天，我跟着杰克·卡瓦列罗斯去了英特尔的研究实验室，参观一些还不到上节点阶段的前沿想法。

272
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:50,320
We started with wiring. A transistor is only useful once it’s connected to others, and on a modern chip that takes more than a dozen layers of metal wire stacked
我们从布线开始。晶体管只有与其他元件连接后才有用，而在现代芯片上，这需要在晶体管上方堆叠超过十层金属导线，

273
00:29:50,320 --> 00:30:01,360
above the transistors, called interconnect. Those wires are made of copper, and as they shrink, copper starts to run into trouble. Intel is exploring a replacement: ruthenium.
称为互连。这些导线由铜制成，但随着尺寸缩小，铜开始出现问题。英特尔正在探索一种替代方案：钌。

274
00:30:01,360 --> 00:30:05,280
Ruthenium has higher resistivity, so why are you guys exploring ruthenium for the future?
钌的电阻率更高，那你们为什么还要探索用钌来打造未来呢？

275
00:30:05,280 --> 00:30:11,440
Interestingly enough, as you scale the dimensions of those copper wires, you run into challenges.
有趣的是，当这些铜线的尺寸不断增大时，你会遇到各种挑战。

276
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:17,680
For example, copper typically requires a liner in order to deposit the copper to fill those lines.
例如，为了将铜填充到这些线路中，通常需要先铺设一层衬垫。

277
00:30:17,680 --> 00:30:23,680
Ruthenium is very different in that regard because it’s a material that we can actually etch.
钌在这方面非常不同，因为它是一种我们可以实际进行刻蚀的材料。

278
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:37,600
As such, by etching it, we don’t need that liner. Now you scale those very fine dimensions, and if you can trade off that resistive liner with no liner, ruthenium starts to win.
因此，通过刻蚀，我们就不需要那层阻隔层了。当尺寸缩小到非常精细的程度时，若能用无阻隔层替代有阻隔层，钌的优势就开始显现了。

279
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:46,960
There is a crossover point where ruthenium will have much better conductivity, better resistivity than copper. That’s roughly in the 20-nanometer pitch range,
当钌的导电性和电阻率优于铜时，就达到了一个交叉点，这大约出现在20纳米的间距范围内。也就是说，

280
00:30:46,960 --> 00:30:55,680
which means, if you can imagine, half of the pitch is your metal line, and the other half is the dielectric that isolates them. So what is the liner?
你可以想象，间距的一半是金属线，另一半是隔离它们的介电材料。那么，这里的“衬层”指的是什么？

281
00:30:56,960 --> 00:31:05,920
Think of it almost like a glue layer or transition layer that we need in order to deposit the copper and have it stick, and have it make that connection to the copper lines below it.
可以把它想象成一种类似粘合层或过渡层，我们需要它来沉积铜，让铜能够附着，并与下方的铜线形成有效连接。

282
00:31:05,920 --> 00:31:10,400
These are all interlocking copper lines that interconnect to each other.
这些都是相互连接的铜线，彼此之间相互连通。

283
00:31:10,400 --> 00:31:15,680
But every time you deposit copper, you’ve always had a liner underneath it.
但每次你沉积铜时，下面始终都有一个衬层。

284
00:31:15,680 --> 00:31:24,480
In the past, that liner may have been a significant portion of the entirety of that conduction path. But over time, we’ve scaled it, made it thinner,
过去，这条导线可能占整个导电路径的相当大一部分。但随着时间推移，我们将其缩小、变薄，

285
00:31:24,480 --> 00:31:35,760
and so copper comes back into play as a contender. One thing I’d be curious to know is how you decide that a technology is mature and ready for high-volume manufacturing.
铜又重新成为有力的竞争者。我很好奇的是，你们是如何判断一项技术已经成熟，可以投入大规模生产的？

286
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:40,640
With 10 nanometer, you were mentioning you guys moved on from copper to cobalt,
10纳米制程时，你们提到从铜换成了钴，

287
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:48,960
and there were potential issues with that. How do you decide that this technology is going to work at scale? The integration of these materials into such
但可能存在一些问题。你们是如何判断这项技术能够实现规模化应用的？将这些材料整合进晶体管生

288
00:31:48,960 --> 00:31:54,800
a complex system as the transistor ecosystem involves many, many different components.
态系统这样复杂的系统中，涉及了众多不同的组件。

289
00:31:54,800 --> 00:31:59,440
The scalability is one part, but its ability to integrate is another.
可扩展性是一方面，而其集成能力是另一方面。

290
00:31:59,440 --> 00:32:02,800
At the time we made a decision for cobalt, it was the forerunner.
当时我们决定采用钴时，它还是先行者。

291
00:32:02,800 --> 00:32:07,520
The results were stronger at the time. But as we work with our ecosystem externally,
当时的结果更偏向于铜。但随着我们与外部生态系统合作，

292
00:32:07,520 --> 00:32:20,480
the vendor ecosystem that provides these materials and the tools that we integrate them with, we find solutions down the road that may bring copper back in line as being the stronger of the two.
包括提供这些材料和与我们集成工具的供应商生态，我们发现未来可能会有解决方案，让铜重新成为两者中更强的选项。

293
00:32:20,480 --> 00:32:24,720
That’s exactly what happens. Every technology that we go through,
这正是实际情况。我们每接触一项新技术，

294
00:32:24,720 --> 00:32:35,760
we will evaluate multiple options. As we get closer to our time horizon for delivering a manufacturing-worthy product, we have to make a decision.
都会评估多种选择。当我们越来越接近交付具备量产能力产品的时限时，就必须做出决定。

295
00:32:35,760 --> 00:32:39,440
The leading option at the time was cobalt. Now it’s copper again,
当时主流的选择是钴，现在又回到了铜，

296
00:32:39,440 --> 00:32:50,880
and perhaps in the future it’ll be ruthenium. I’d love to get your thoughts on CFET, basically stacking the transistors on top of each other. What is progress like there?
也许未来会用到钌。我很想听听你对CFET的看法，也就是把晶体管堆叠在一起。那里的进展如何？

297
00:32:50,880 --> 00:32:53,120
Can you keep making more and more layers? Is it just two?
你能一直增加越来越多的层吗？就只有两层吗？

298
00:32:53,120 --> 00:32:55,440
Can you go three, four? How high can you get?
能再往上三、四吗？你能爬多高？

299
00:32:55,440 --> 00:33:03,200
That evaluation of CFET is currently underway. Not just by us, by our competition.
对CFET的评估目前正在展开，不仅是我们，竞争对手也在进行。

300
00:33:03,200 --> 00:33:07,600
Labs externally are looking at it. It is the ultimate in stackability.
外部的实验室正在研究它。它在可堆叠性方面达到了极致。

301
00:33:07,600 --> 00:33:15,360
We’re taking transistors that had to be compressed more and more, and we’re relaxing that in some ways because we’re going to stack them on top of each other.
我们正在处理那些需要不断压缩的晶体管，现在我们会在某些方面放宽这一要求，因为我们将把它们堆叠在一起。

302
00:33:15,360 --> 00:33:18,640
But the act of stacking does not mean that they’re not interconnected.
但堆叠本身并不意味着它们之间没有连接。

303
00:33:18,640 --> 00:33:27,840
What used to be connections on the top or even on the bottom, as we’ve seen from our press release, now has to go vertically in between the transistors.
过去，连接通常位于顶部甚至底部，但根据我们发布的消息，现在这些连接必须垂直穿过晶体管之间。

304
00:33:27,840 --> 00:33:37,520
That’s a new challenge because the aspect ratio of creating trenches and filling those trenches becomes more and more difficult. It’s not as straightforward as stacking the
这带来了新的挑战，因为沟槽的深宽比越来越高，填充沟槽也变得越来越困难。这不像堆叠晶体管那样直接，

305
00:33:37,520 --> 00:33:40,560
transistors, because you still have to think about interconnecting them.
因为你仍需考虑如何连接它们。

306
00:33:40,560 --> 00:33:45,280
Is the issue there the etch to get that deep hole? Aspect ratio for the etches is a big part of it.
问题出在刻蚀上，要实现这么深的孔。刻蚀的深宽比是其中关键因素。

307
00:33:45,280 --> 00:33:54,480
We’re working with our vendors to evolve on that and to improve that, but it’s also what you fill those very tiny spaces with. Because filling can sometimes leave
我们正与供应商合作，推动这方面的发展和改进，但同样重要的是，你如何填充那些极其微小的空间。因为填充时有时会留下气隙或孔洞，

308
00:33:54,480 --> 00:33:58,960
air gaps or holes that are never really filled, and that’s not ideal.
这些地方实际上从未被完全填满，这并不是理想的状态。

309
00:33:58,960 --> 00:34:02,000
I assume there’s a version of CFET that works inside this lab.
我猜这个实验室里应该有一种能工作的 CFET 版本。

310
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:06,000
What is the kind of wafer or process that is working right now?
现在正在使用的晶圆或制程是什么类型？

311
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:07,648
Then what’s the next step? What’s the next step after that,
接下来的下一步是什么？在进入全规模量产流程之前，

312
00:34:08,160 --> 00:34:20,720
until it’s inside the full high-volume process? Part of the process of evolving these technologies, especially something more complex like an architecture — not just a basic chemistry
我们还需要走哪些步骤？在推动这些技术演进的过程中，尤其是像CFET这样更复杂的架构——不仅仅是基础的化学或材料，

313
00:34:20,720 --> 00:34:30,880
or a material, but a full architecture like CFET — is that we have to make short-loop vehicles that can give us faster turnaround. The normal manufacturing process can
而是一个完整的架构——我们必须打造能够实现快速迭代的短周期载体。常规的制造流程无法满足这一需求。

314
00:34:30,880 --> 00:34:35,680
take a month or longer to complete the thousands of steps that it goes through.
完成它所经历的数千个步骤可能需要一个月甚至更长时间。

315
00:34:35,680 --> 00:34:41,760
Our goal here is to take unit cells of that process and qualify them.
我们的目标是获取该工艺的单元结构并对其进行验证。

316
00:34:41,760 --> 00:34:52,880
We take advantage of short-loop vehicles that can give us electrical parameters, chemical parameters that can resolve some of those challenges, those speed bumps.
我们利用短循环车辆，获取电气参数和化学参数，从而解决部分挑战和瓶颈问题。

317
00:34:52,880 --> 00:34:58,800
Once we’ve resolved them, then we combine them into a more complex test vehicle.
解决这些问题后，我们再将它们整合到一个更复杂的测试车辆中。

318
00:34:58,800 --> 00:35:01,760
What you would find in here is something very simple.
你在这里会发现一些非常简单的东西。

319
00:35:01,760 --> 00:35:08,720
You might be making a diode, you might be making a capacitor, which are components of the transistor.
你可能在制造二极管，也可能在制造电容器，这些都是晶体管的组成部分。

320
00:35:08,720 --> 00:35:20,800
As those evolve and become stronger, better performing, you combine them to make the next stage, which might be the transistor itself. In the CFET example, you might not choose to
随着这些结构不断演进并变得更强、性能更好，你就可以将它们组合起来，进入下一个阶段，比如晶体管本身。以CFET为例，

321
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:25,840
do four ribbons on top of four ribbons. You might just choose to do one on one,
你可能不会选择把四条细条叠在四条细条之上，而更可能选择一对一的结构，

322
00:35:25,840 --> 00:35:35,360
because naturally the aspect ratio of dealing with something that much taller becomes more and more challenging. So we would have a simplified version of a CFET,
因为处理这种更高结构时，其长宽比会变得越来越具挑战性。因此，我们会采用一种简化的CFET版本。

323
00:35:35,360 --> 00:35:40,560
which has only one wire for the NMOS and one wire for the PMOS.
这仅有一根NMOS的连线和一根PMOS的连线。

324
00:35:40,560 --> 00:35:49,520
That simplifies our job, but it still helps us evaluate the stacking of devices and how they will interconnect. But are the feature sizes here as
这简化了我们的工作，但仍有助于评估器件的堆叠方式及其互连效果。

325
00:35:49,520 --> 00:35:52,080
small as they will be eventually? Or are the feature sizes bigger?
但这里的特征尺寸最终会更小吗？还是说它们会更大？

326
00:35:52,080 --> 00:36:03,840
Oh, absolutely. In some cases, at least as small. For example, you’ve heard of 2D materials, these transition layer dichalcogenides, as they’re called.
当然，有些情况下甚至更小。比如，你可能听说过二维材料，也就是所谓的过渡层二硫属化合物。

327
00:36:03,840 --> 00:36:12,320
They’re very, very thin materials. They’re essentially one monolayer of atoms that we deal with. Those depositions can happen in this lab.
它们是非常非常薄的材料，本质上就是我们所处理的单层原子。这些沉积过程可以在本实验室完成。

328
00:36:12,320 --> 00:36:22,400
We can evaluate those films in terms of conductivity, our ability to contact them, our ability to modulate that conductivity, which is what the transistor does.
我们可以从导电性、我们与它们接触的能力以及调控这种导电性的能力来评估这些材料，而这正是晶体管所实现的功能。

329
00:36:22,400 --> 00:36:30,480
So yes, we do have the ability to not only deposit films that are very small and thin to the
是的，我们确实具备不仅沉积原子级薄的薄膜，

330
00:36:30,480 --> 00:36:42,640
atomic scale, but also print very small features. We have EUV technology, or E-beam technology, that can allow us to print very small features similar to what we will eventually make in production.
还能打印极小特征的能力。我们拥有 EUV 或电子束技术，能够打印出与最终量产产品相似的微小特征。

331
00:36:43,280 --> 00:36:51,840
I guess I don’t understand intuitively, lacking the context in this process,
我猜我直觉上无法理解，缺乏这个过程的背景知识，

332
00:36:51,840 --> 00:36:56,960
if you have the features that are as small for a working CFET right now.
如果现在要实现一个正常工作的CFET，其特征尺寸已经小到这种程度了。

333
00:36:57,520 --> 00:37:00,480
Why will it take 10 years for it to be in my phone?
为什么得等10年才能放进我的手机里？

334
00:37:01,520 --> 00:37:05,520
I understand that there’s a process, but why is it such a long process?
我明白有个流程，但为什么这么漫长呢？

335
00:37:05,520 --> 00:37:14,320
The answer is slightly different when you’re dealing with an architecture like CFET versus a novel material. Materials have, of course, the
当你面对的是像CFET这样的架构，而不是一种新材料时，答案会略有不同。

336
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:26,400
challenges of being deposited, etched, removed. Their compatibility with other layers above and below, all those things have to be researched first before you put them in a production line.
材料当然存在沉积、刻蚀、去除等方面的挑战，还需研究其与上下层材料的兼容性等问题，这些都必须在投入生产线之前先行解决。

337
00:37:26,400 --> 00:37:34,800
An architecture may seem like a simple evolution. “Oh, you’re making nanoribbon transistors next to each other. What’s wrong with just stacking them?”
一种架构看起来可能只是简单的演进。“哦，你只是把纳米带晶体管并排排列而已。为什么不直接堆叠呢？”

338
00:37:35,360 --> 00:37:41,680
But the act of stacking them, of course, increases the aspect ratio for etches, depositions, cleans,
但堆叠它们的过程无疑会增加刻蚀、沉积和清洗的深宽比，

339
00:37:41,680 --> 00:37:52,560
and the technology may simply not be ready yet. But if you’ve already made it here, there’s a version of the technology that’s ready, right? Correct. We’ve made a simplified version that
而这项技术可能尚未准备就绪。不过，如果你已经走到这一步，那就说明有某种技术版本已经成熟，对吧？没错。我们已经开发出一种简化版本，

340
00:37:52,560 --> 00:38:00,160
may not be an advantage over our current side-by-side technology of nanoribbons,
它可能在性能上还无法超越当前的纳米带并排技术。

341
00:38:00,160 --> 00:38:10,320
but we need to evolve it to make it maybe two nanoribbons, or to add the right type of metals and dielectrics, in order to extract the maximum performance out of that device.
但我们必须对其进行改进，比如将其变为两条纳米带，或添加合适的金属和电介质，以充分挖掘该器件的性能潜力。

342
00:38:10,320 --> 00:38:20,560
Got it. So it’s like a long tail of— There’s a thousand different steps that all have to come together in a very unique way to make the performance compatible or actually
明白了。这就像一个长尾效应——有上千个不同的步骤，必须以非常独特的方式协同配合，才能让性能达到兼容，

343
00:38:20,560 --> 00:38:23,600
supersede the prior technology. Where do the ideas tend to fail?
甚至超越前代技术。这些想法通常在哪儿会失败？

344
00:38:24,480 --> 00:38:37,840
You have 20 ideas like 2D materials. What is the way in which you decide this is actually not worth pursuing further? Sometimes we discover fundamentals about the
你有二十个类似二维材料的设想，你是如何判断其中哪些其实不值得继续推进的？有时我们会发现材料本身的一些基本特性，

345
00:38:37,840 --> 00:38:42,800
material that will be incompatible with the rest of the processing that we do.
与我们后续的工艺流程不兼容。

346
00:38:44,320 --> 00:38:48,320
The first level of experiments may simply be very simple depositions.
第一阶段的实验可能只是非常简单的沉积。

347
00:38:48,320 --> 00:38:53,040
You say, “Okay, I can deposit the film, I can look at it, I can measure it.
你说：“好，我可以把薄膜沉积上去，可以观察它，可以测量它。”

348
00:38:53,040 --> 00:38:56,720
But the moment I try to make a contact to it, nothing sticks.”
但只要我试图与它接触，就什么也粘不上。

349
00:38:56,720 --> 00:39:00,560
It could be something as fundamental as the adhesion.
它可能像粘附性这样基本的因素。

350
00:39:00,560 --> 00:39:06,400
The next step in the process simply has no way to adhere to the material.
下一步工艺根本无法让材料附着。

351
00:39:06,400 --> 00:39:10,320
So you may rule out a material because of something as simple as that.
所以你可能仅仅因为这么简单的原因就排除某种材料。

352
00:39:10,880 --> 00:39:23,600
More complex challenges might be that the subsequent processing may have gaseous interactions with downstream steps. For example, I’ve deposited the film, I’ve capped
更复杂的问题可能是，后续的工艺步骤可能与下游工序产生气体相互作用。例如，我已经沉积了薄膜，并用其他材料进行了封装，

353
00:39:23,600 --> 00:39:34,080
it with other materials, but they’re not hermetic. So my third or fourth or fifth step in the process may actually still interact with it and change its properties.
但这些封装并不完全密封。因此，我工艺流程中的第三、第四或第五步仍可能与之发生相互作用，从而改变其特性。

354
00:39:34,080 --> 00:39:40,320
That makes it unstable, and now we have to find better hermetic sealing for that process.
这会导致其不稳定，因此我们必须为该工艺找到更优的气密性密封方案。

355
00:39:40,320 --> 00:39:45,760
If we don’t find a way to control it, then it’s a dead end for us.
如果我们找不到控制它的方法，那对我们来说就是一条死路。

356
00:39:45,760 --> 00:39:55,200
Stacking transistors is still in the research lab. Stacking whole chips is already in production, and it tackles a different problem: how separate chips communicate with each other.
堆叠晶体管仍处于实验室研究阶段，而堆叠整个芯片已进入量产，解决的是另一个问题：如何让独立的芯片相互通信。

357
00:39:55,200 --> 00:39:59,760
Modern computers are divided into multiple parts, each optimized for a different task.
现代计算机被划分为多个部分，每个部分都针对不同任务进行了优化。

358
00:39:59,760 --> 00:40:10,400
For example, Nvidia’s Blackwell data-center GPU joins two huge processor chips and surrounds them with eight stacks of high-bandwidth memory. These chips have to constantly send
例如，英伟达的Blackwell数据中心GPU将两颗巨大的处理器芯片连接在一起，并在周围环绕了八堆高带宽内存。

359
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:20,000
data back and forth with each other. And in AI, the speed of that traffic, not the raw computation itself, is often what limits how fast the workload finishes.
这些芯片必须持续不断地相互传输数据。在AI领域，这种数据传输的速度，而非原始计算能力，往往才是决定任务完成快慢的关键因素。

360
00:40:20,000 --> 00:40:24,000
Imagine two chips side by side, communicating through their facing edges.
想象两块芯片并排放置，通过相对的边缘进行通信。

361
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:33,520
If you double each chip’s width and length, you’ll have four times the area for circuitry, but only twice the edge length for connections. This is the area-versus-perimeter problem.
如果你将每个芯片的宽度和长度都翻倍，电路布局的面积会变为原来的四倍，但连接的边长仅增加一倍。这就是面积与周长的矛盾问题。

362
00:40:33,520 --> 00:40:42,160
As transistors become denser, a chip’s ability to do computations increases faster than its ability to move data around. The industry has known about this problem
随着晶体管越来越密集，芯片进行计算的能力提升速度，远超其传输数据的能力。这个问题业界已知晓多年，

363
00:40:42,160 --> 00:40:45,600
for years, so multiple technologies have been invented to address it.
因此已发明多种技术来应对。

364
00:40:45,600 --> 00:40:51,440
The completed circuits must undergo two last operations: bonding and packaging.
完成的电路必须经过最后两个工序：键合和封装。

365
00:40:51,440 --> 00:40:56,320
Once a chip is made, it still has to be connected to other chips and to the rest of the computer.
芯片制造完成后，仍需与其他芯片以及计算机的其他部分连接。

366
00:40:56,320 --> 00:41:00,240
That step is called packaging, and it has changed a lot in the last few decades.
这一步叫做封装，过去几十年里它发生了巨大变化。

367
00:41:00,240 --> 00:41:04,320
At a high level, you can think of the evolution of packaging like this: initially,
从宏观层面来看，封装技术的演进可以这样理解：最初，

368
00:41:04,320 --> 00:41:15,040
chips were laid out side by side. Wires connecting them went out the side of one chip to another. To allow for more communication density, the industry introduced a shared piece of silicon, called an
芯片是并排摆放的，连接它们的导线从一个芯片侧面延伸到另一个芯片。为了提升通信密度，业界引入了一块共享的硅片，

369
00:41:15,040 --> 00:41:25,440
interposer, for multiple chips to sit on. A chip can talk to other chips through the wiring that runs directly through the interposer. This type of packaging is often called “2.5D”.
称为中介层，多个芯片可以放置在上面。芯片之间通过贯穿中介层的导线直接通信。这种封装方式通常被称为“2.5D”。

370
00:41:25,440 --> 00:41:30,400
Another way to increase connection density is to bond chips directly to one another.
另一种提高连接密度的方法是将芯片直接彼此键合。

371
00:41:30,400 --> 00:41:41,680
This allows for shorter connections between chips, but the early versions of this idea used solder bumps, which are bulky and increased resistance. It would be better to skip the solder and fuse
这使得芯片之间的连接更短，但早期的这种设计使用了焊锡凸块，体积大且增加了电阻。更好的方法是跳过焊锡，

372
00:41:41,680 --> 00:41:45,520
the signal wire connections of the two chips directly to each other.
直接将两个芯片的信号线连接起来。

373
00:41:45,520 --> 00:41:53,600
This is called hybrid bonding, and it is one of the technologies the semiconductor industry is investing in the most. Hybrid bonding works by aligning the copper
这被称为混合键合，是半导体行业投资最多的技術之一。混合键合通过將一塊晶片的銅互連與另

374
00:41:53,600 --> 00:42:01,280
interconnects of one chip with those of another. Then these chips are pushed together and heated so their interconnects expand and bridge the gap between the chips.
一塊晶片的銅互連對齊，然後將晶片推到一起並加熱，使互連膨脹，從而跨越晶片之間的間隙。

375
00:42:01,280 --> 00:42:05,280
This process makes the insulating surfaces around the copper bond as well.
这一工艺同样会在铜焊点周围形成绝缘表面。

376
00:42:05,280 --> 00:42:09,120
And so by removing the solder bumps, you end up with much denser connections.
因此，通过去除焊点，你能获得更密集的连接。

377
00:42:09,120 --> 00:42:16,960
But the reason this technology is only now entering volume manufacturing is because it took a while to iron out the alignment and defect challenges that it introduced.
但这项技术直到现在才进入批量生产，是因为需要一段时间来解决它带来的对齐和缺陷难题。

378
00:42:16,960 --> 00:42:25,440
In the packaging lab, I asked Tyler Osborn, a director in Intel’s advanced packaging technology development group, how they put this into practice.
在封装实验室，我问了英特尔先进封装技术开发部门的总监泰勒·奥斯本，他们是如何将这一技术付诸实践的。

379
00:42:25,440 --> 00:42:28,880
Intel has spent over 50 years optimizing its CPUs for speed.
英特尔已投入超过50年时间，持续优化其CPU的运行速度。

380
00:42:28,880 --> 00:42:36,720
But lots of these optimizations—like caching and branch prediction and speculative execution—reduce predictability. The exact same instructions can take four
但许多优化手段——比如缓存、分支预测和推测执行——都会降低可预测性。同样的指令，一次可能只需四个周期，

381
00:42:36,720 --> 00:42:45,840
cycles to execute one time and 300 the next. When you stack billions of instructions together and execute them across multiple threads and cores, these non-determinisms compound into
下一次却需要三百个周期。当你将数十亿条指令组合起来，并在多个线程和核心上执行时，这些非确定性因素会叠加，

382
00:42:45,840 --> 00:42:49,520
all sorts of hard-to-reproduce bugs. If you work on serious systems,
产生各种难以复现的错误。如果你从事的是关键系统开发，

383
00:42:49,520 --> 00:43:01,440
you’ve definitely experienced this. Antithesis solved this by doing something a bit crazy: they built a 100% deterministic computer. They identified every single door in Intel’s x86
你肯定经历过这种情况。反题公司通过一个相当疯狂的举措解决了这个问题：他们打造了一台100%确定性的计算机。他们识别出英特尔x86

384
00:43:01,440 --> 00:43:05,760
architecture where non-determinism could leak in—network packets, disk reads,
架构中每一个可能导致非确定性泄露的接口——网络包、

385
00:43:05,760 --> 00:43:15,360
temperature sensors, even time itself. And they either welded each of these shut or slotted in a controlled simulation. This deterministic computer is just a foundation.
磁盘读取、温度传感器，甚至时间本身。然后他们要么把这些接口全部焊死，要么插入可控的模拟器。这台确定性计算机仅仅是一个基础。

386
00:43:15,360 --> 00:43:22,720
On top of it, Antithesis built a testing platform that compresses years of production hardening into minutes. It runs your software through thousands of
此外，反题公司构建了一个测试平台，能将数年的生产强化测试压缩至几分钟内完成。该平台会将你的软件运行在成千上万的并行世界中，

387
00:43:22,720 --> 00:43:30,560
parallel worlds, injecting faults and intelligently steering each one towards a one-in-a-billion failure that you’d never be able to test yourself.
注入故障，并智能引导每个世界走向那种百万分之一概率才会出现的故障，这种故障你根本无法自行测试。

388
00:43:30,560 --> 00:43:38,080
This determinism is what’s behind all the other insane Antithesis features that I’ve mentioned over the past few episodes. It’s what lets you reproduce any bug
这种确定性正是我之前几期节目中提到的所有疯狂反论功能背后的核心。它让你能够复现任何漏洞，并在测试运行中任意时间点进行回溯。

389
00:43:38,080 --> 00:43:53,200
and time travel to any point in the test run. Most importantly, it’s what lets you—or your agents—trace every failure back to its root cause. Go to antithesis.com/dwarkesh to learn more.
最重要的是，它让你或你的智能体能够将每一次失败追溯到根本原因。访问 antithesis.com/dwarkesh 了解更多信息。

390
00:43:53,200 --> 00:43:58,400
Two different ways you could do hybrid bonding: die to die and die to wafer.
实现混合键合的两种不同方式：芯片对芯片和芯片对晶圆。

391
00:43:59,200 --> 00:44:03,200
Maybe you can talk about the advantages of both and which one actually practically works better.
也许你可以谈谈两者的优点，以及哪一个在实际应用中效果更好。

392
00:44:03,200 --> 00:44:07,280
Sure. Around Intel, what I think you’re referring to as die to die,
好的。关于英特尔，您所说的晶粒对晶粒，

393
00:44:07,280 --> 00:44:14,160
we typically call wafer to wafer. We actually take a full wafer and another wafer and bond them together. That’s an approach that’s really popular
我们通常称之为晶圆对晶圆。我们实际上是将整片晶圆与另一片晶圆直接键合在一起。这种方法在CMOS图像传

394
00:44:14,160 --> 00:44:19,120
right now in the CMOS image sensor industry. In fact, it basically dominates it.
感器行业目前非常流行，事实上几乎已经主导了整个领域。

395
00:44:19,120 --> 00:44:21,840
It’s being looked at for other things like memory stacking.
它还被考虑用于其他用途，比如内存堆叠。

396
00:44:22,640 --> 00:44:24,640
People have published ideas about all kinds of stuff.
人们已经发表过各种各样的想法。

397
00:44:24,640 --> 00:44:35,120
One of the challenges with that, though, is that you need dies that perfectly match each other, and are perfectly left-handed and right-handed, so you have to be able to bond exactly one to the other.
不过，其中一个挑战是，你需要晶片完全匹配，且一个为左手型，一个为右手型，必须能够精确地将它们键合在一起。

398
00:44:35,120 --> 00:44:43,040
One-to-one die size is a challenge. If they’re not the exact same design device on top and bottom, that’s a problem. With wafer to wafer, it’s challenging to make
单芯片尺寸匹配是个挑战。如果上下两层芯片的设计不完全一致，就会出现问题。而采用晶圆对晶圆的方式，

399
00:44:43,040 --> 00:44:46,160
sure that you have wafers that match really well in terms of the sort.
要确保晶圆之间的匹配度非常高，难度也很大。

400
00:44:46,160 --> 00:44:49,280
So sort good array here versus sort good array here.
所以这里排序好的数组， versus 这里排序好的数组。

401
00:44:49,280 --> 00:44:57,760
If they match exactly — good die and bad die all match — I can get 100% yield of that operation of the good dies. But the chances of that are very low.
如果完全匹配——良品和坏品都完全一致——我就能实现良品该工序的100%良率。但这种情况发生的概率非常低。

402
00:44:57,760 --> 00:45:01,600
There’s going to be some randomization of the two, and whatever that randomization is,
两者之间会存在一定的随机性，这种随机性会导致良品与良品的结

403
00:45:01,600 --> 00:45:06,960
will be loss of good bonding to bad. Whereas with chip to wafer,
合变成良品与不良品的结合。而采用芯片到晶圆的方案，

404
00:45:06,960 --> 00:45:16,640
I can dictate the terms. I can singulate and have known good dies that I’ve tested at the chip level, and then only bond known good to a known good base position.
我可以掌控一切。我可以进行单个化处理，并使用在芯片级别已经测试过的良品，仅将良品与良品的基底位置进行绑定。

405
00:45:16,640 --> 00:45:19,760
Do you think then chip to wafer is the way that you’ll do hybrid bonding in the future?
你认为未来的混合键合会采用芯片到晶圆的方式吗？

406
00:45:19,760 --> 00:45:23,120
I think we’re going to do both. We definitely have the capability to do both.
我认为我们两者都会做。我们确实具备同时做到这两点的能力。

407
00:45:23,120 --> 00:45:31,200
It’s really customer and architecture driven, but certainly chip to wafer in the logic-to-logic space makes a lot of sense. You were explaining that going from advanced
这实际上是由客户和架构驱动的，但显然在逻辑到逻辑的芯片到晶圆层面，确实很有意义。你刚才提到，

408
00:45:31,200 --> 00:45:34,560
packaging to hybrid bonding, the size of the pitches is decreasing.
从先进封装到混合键合，其间距尺寸正在缩小。

409
00:45:34,560 --> 00:45:37,360
How much further could that go? That’s a wonderful question.
还能走多远？这是个很棒的问题。

410
00:45:38,000 --> 00:45:46,080
Historically, like we were saying, 15 years ago first-level interconnect was 130-150 micrometer pitch. Today in HVM, we’re doing 25
历史上，正如我们所说，15年前的一级互连间距是130到150微米。如今在高产量制造中，我们已经在Fov

411
00:45:46,080 --> 00:45:50,400
micrometer pitch in Foveros. Here we’re doing sub-10,
eros工艺上做到25微米的间距。

412
00:45:50,400 --> 00:45:58,400
but there’s already been literature out there. In fact, the biggest conference every year in packaging is the IEEE ECTC conference that happens in May.
而这里我们已经实现低于10微米，不过相关文献早已出现。事实上，每年包装领域最大的会议就是五月份举行的IEEE ECTC会议。

413
00:45:59,120 --> 00:46:08,800
There was a paper there this year showing reliability data on a 0.45 micron pitch hybrid bonded device. So you can imagine that’s a whole other
今年有一篇论文展示了0.45微米节距的混合键合器件的可靠性数据。你可以想象，

414
00:46:08,800 --> 00:46:13,440
order of magnitude in density again from where we are right now in HVM.
这比我们目前在量产中的密度又提升了一个全新的数量级。

415
00:46:14,160 --> 00:46:18,800
That scalability has already been proven. So I see a long runway there.
这种可扩展性已经得到验证，因此我看到那里有很长的发展空间。

416
00:46:18,800 --> 00:46:23,280
Within interconnect, within a single wafer, how far separated are they usually?
在互连中，在单个晶圆上，它们通常相隔多远？

417
00:46:23,280 --> 00:46:35,600
In the back end of the fab, you can imagine the few layers below packaging are typically around one micron to a half micron in size. So if you can push packaging down
在晶圆厂的后段工艺中，你可以想象封装下方的几层通常尺寸在1微米到0.5微米之间。因此，如果你能把封装工艺推进到0.

418
00:46:35,600 --> 00:46:46,160
to 0.5 microns or below, you can almost think about taking layers off the top of the die and connecting a lower layer within the silicon to another lower layer within the silicon.
5微米甚至更低，就可以几乎相当于把芯片顶部的几层去掉，直接将硅片内部的一个低层连接到另一个低层。

419
00:46:46,720 --> 00:46:49,680
That’s really where the beauty of hybrid bonding happens.
这正是混合键合技术魅力所在。

420
00:46:49,680 --> 00:46:59,520
You could think someday about even having reduced interconnect layers on both sides that we can bond together and have less total IR drop between the two transistor layers.
你可以设想，未来或许能在两侧都减少互连层，将它们键合在一起，从而降低两层晶体管之间的总IR压降。

421
00:46:59,520 --> 00:47:04,160
It’s really cool to think about where that could go once we get into those regimes.
一旦我们进入那些领域，想想它会走向何方，真的很酷。

422
00:47:04,160 --> 00:47:11,120
Actually, I am very curious about… For AI, it makes sense why hybrid bonding is so powerful, because then you can get higher bandwidth to the memory.
实际上，我很好奇……对于AI来说，混合键合之所以如此强大，原因在于它能实现更高的内存带宽。

423
00:47:11,120 --> 00:47:14,080
But you were saying there are issues with heat and everything.
但你说过，散热和其他方面都有问题。

424
00:47:14,080 --> 00:47:17,840
What is the advantage for non-AI applications of hybrid bonding?
混合键合技术对非AI应用有何优势？

425
00:47:17,840 --> 00:47:28,080
Excellent question. There are companies out there that are looking at it for non-AI applications, even in client devices. One of the big things I think it can help
非常好的问题。有一些公司正在探索它在非AI应用中的潜力，甚至在客户端设备中也是如此。我认为其中一个重要优势是，

426
00:47:28,080 --> 00:47:37,920
with there is, number one, obviously the same raw interconnect density, especially between something like a CPU and a GPU. But the other one is around power.
首先，显然它能提供更高的原始互连密度，尤其是在CPU和GPU之间。其次，它在功耗方面也有显著优势。

427
00:47:37,920 --> 00:47:46,000
Like we talked about before, as you get to finer and finer interconnects, you can reduce the total power consumption, in picojoules per bit, for that interface.
正如我们之前讨论的，随着互连越来越精细，你可以降低该接口每比特的总功耗，单位为皮焦耳。

428
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:52,720
With hybrid bonding in those applications, you could think about that as another way that packaging can help reduce the total system power budget.
在这些应用中采用混合键合技术，可以看作是封装另一种降低系统总功耗的方式。

429
00:47:53,680 --> 00:48:02,960
That gives you lots of benefits: lower power, less heat, for the same benefit in terms of actual electrical performance. So far, this has been about making better chips.
这能带来诸多好处：在保持实际电性能不变的前提下，功耗更低、发热更少。到目前为止，这都是关于如何制造更好的芯片。

430
00:48:02,960 --> 00:48:11,040
The other half of the question is how many chips the industry can make if AI labs are willing to pay for far more of them. I’m getting a chance to talk to
另一个问题是，如果AI实验室愿意支付更高价格，产业能生产多少芯片。我有机会与英特尔首席技术官

431
00:48:11,040 --> 00:48:20,400
Pushkar Ranade, who is the CTO of Intel. I’m very interested in understanding how the fact that the entire industry has to rely on the same suppliers bottlenecks
普什卡尔·拉纳德交谈，非常想了解整个行业依赖相同供应商这一事实，如何制约了你们扩大生产的速度。

432
00:48:20,400 --> 00:48:28,080
your ability to ramp up production. For this EUV tool, ASML produces fewer than 100 of them a year. They don’t plan to produce more
以这款EUV设备为例，阿斯麦每年生产的数量不到100台，他们目前没有计划增加产量。

433
00:48:28,080 --> 00:48:29,840
than 100 a year, or significantly more, even by the end of the decade.
每年超过100，甚至可能多得多，即便到本十年末也是如此。

434
00:48:31,360 --> 00:48:40,400
That has to be split between you, between TSMC, between all the memory makers who are ramping up production, like SK Hynix. How does that limit your ability to scale up to all this demand? It is a limiter.
这必须在你们、台积电以及像SK海力士这样正在扩大生产的内存厂商之间分配。这如何限制了你们满足如此巨大需求的能力？这是一个制约因素。

435
00:48:42,080 --> 00:48:45,840
We have to make the most of the capacity that we already have installed.
我们必须充分利用已安装的产能。

436
00:48:45,840 --> 00:48:50,080
We’ll try to increase the output from our existing fleet of tools.
我们将努力提升现有工具集群的产出。

437
00:48:50,080 --> 00:49:03,200
But EUV or ASML is not the only bottleneck. It is definitely a bigger bottleneck than some of the other tools, but to a large extent, we’re seeing
但极紫外光刻（EUV）或阿斯麦（ASML）并非唯一的瓶颈。尽管它比其他一些设备的瓶颈更严重，但从整体来看，

438
00:49:04,000 --> 00:49:17,680
all equipment seeing tremendous supply shortage. Even though there are multiple providers of other tools, once you lock into a process flow — let’s say 18A, which is a mature process
我们正面临所有设备供应极度短缺的问题。尽管其他设备有多个供应商，但一旦你确定了某个工艺流程——比如如今已成熟的18A工

439
00:49:17,680 --> 00:49:27,760
now — we need a specific tool. We can’t just go and get a different tool for the same process. So we’re locked into that one tool,
艺——就需要特定的设备。你无法随意更换同一流程的其他设备。因此，我们只能依赖这一款设备。

440
00:49:27,760 --> 00:49:36,160
one supplier, and we rely on that. What lessons does this give for 14A in terms of how you structure it? By 2028, it’s going to be such a crazy world.
一个供应商，我们完全依赖它。这给14A的架构设计带来了什么启示？到2028年，世界将变得如此疯狂。

441
00:49:36,160 --> 00:49:39,680
How do you structure things in such a way that you have flexibility in ramping?
你该如何安排，才能在产能爬坡时保持灵活性？

442
00:49:40,640 --> 00:49:48,960
The way to do it — and we’ve been doing it, and generally everybody does it — is you try to maintain as much fungibility as possible going from one node to the next node.
实现这一目标的方法——我们一直在这么做，通常大家也都是这么做的——就是尽可能保持节点之间的可互换性。

443
00:49:48,960 --> 00:49:54,320
Going from 18A to 14A, maybe you would say 80% of the tool set can be reused.
从18A到14A，或许你可以说有80%的设备可以复用。

444
00:49:54,320 --> 00:50:01,280
Then, of course, 14A would require some new capabilities because it’s a denser geometry,
当然，14A工艺需要一些新的能力，因为它采用了更密集的几何结构，

445
00:50:01,280 --> 00:50:11,120
so it would require some unique tools. Do you have a sense of what the ultimate bottleneck is on how many wafers you guys could produce a month, even if you were to decide
因此需要一些独特的工具。你们有没有感觉，即使投入无限资金进行资本开支，你们每月能生产的晶

446
00:50:11,120 --> 00:50:25,360
to invest infinity money into doing CapEx? Given the existing constraints, let’s say we had to maximize the output from a given footprint of tools, then I think it’s incremental, because the
圆数量的最终瓶颈是什么？考虑到现有的限制，假设我们必须在给定的设备占地面积内最大化产出，我认为这只能逐步提升。

447
00:50:25,360 --> 00:50:38,160
process today is already yielding pretty high. If your yield is already pretty high, getting every 1% extra yield is extremely hard because it’s already a finely tuned process.
当前的工艺良率已经相当高了。如果良率本身已经很高，那么再提升1%将非常困难，因为工艺已经调整得非常精细。

448
00:50:38,160 --> 00:50:45,280
Are there enough tools in the world so you could 10X the amount of wafers you’re producing at the leading edge? Not 10X, but probably 2X, yes.
世界上有足够的工具，让你在先进制程下将晶圆产量提升两倍吗？不是十倍，但大概两倍是有的。

449
00:50:45,280 --> 00:50:55,280
That’s quite interesting, because the AI industry for a decade has been 3Xing compute year over year. You’re saying that just can’t continue.
这很有趣，因为过去十年，AI行业每年的算力都在翻三倍。你却说这种情况无法持续下去了。

450
00:50:55,280 --> 00:51:07,200
Unless the entire supply chain — starting from the equipment suppliers, their suppliers, their sub-suppliers — unless everybody produces more, you can’t increase the output.
除非整个供应链——从设备供应商、他们的供应商，再到他们的次级供应商——所有人都增产，否则你无法提高产量。

451
00:51:07,920 --> 00:51:18,400
Just to give the audience a sense of how big the AI demand is and how well it monetizes the products you guys are producing: you can crunch the numbers, and the numbers seem to suggest
仅从观众的角度来看，AI需求有多大，以及你们所生产的产品能带来多高的变现，就能明白：你算一下账就会发现，

452
00:51:18,400 --> 00:51:30,080
that just a couple billion dollars of CapEx on Intel’s account results in enough chips — or the logic for enough chips — to produce, say, $100 billion of revenue for the AI model companies.
英特尔投入的几亿美元资本开支，就能产出足够多的芯片，或具备足够芯片逻辑的产能，足以让AI模型公司实现约1000亿美元的收入。

453
00:51:30,080 --> 00:51:32,720
Of course, there are so many other players within that stack.
当然，这个技术栈中还有许多其他参与者。

454
00:51:32,720 --> 00:51:44,160
There’s also memory and other things. But it’s crazy that a couple billion dollars of CapEx, which can be amortized over many different years, produces $100 billion
还有内存和其他一些东西。但令人震惊的是，几亿美元的资本开支，可以分摊到多年，却能每年带来1000亿美元的收入。

455
00:51:44,160 --> 00:51:52,400
of revenue every single year. That seems to suggest that at some point that should percolate down. At some point, the AI labs could just say,
这似乎说明，总有一天这种效益会传导下去。总有一天，AI实验室只需说一句：

456
00:51:52,400 --> 00:51:54,800
“We’ll just buy everything. Produce as much as you can.
“我们全买下来，能生产多少就生产多少。”

457
00:51:54,800 --> 00:52:04,640
We’ll guarantee future income streams.” There are early signs that could actually be happening already. The demand signals are just so high.
我们将保证未来的收入流。已经有初步迹象表明，这实际上可能已经开始了。需求信号实在太高了。

458
00:52:04,640 --> 00:52:11,760
But I think you were alluding to how the value is accumulating at the top of the technology stack.
但我认为你暗示的是价值正在向技术栈顶端集中。

459
00:52:13,600 --> 00:52:26,960
What is essentially happening is we moved from a fully vertically integrated industry 30-40 years ago, maybe longer, to a very horizontal industry, where you have foundries, you have
本质上，我们从三四十年前，甚至更早，完全垂直整合的产业，转变为如今非常水平化的产业，其中既有代工厂，也有设备制造商及其供应商。

460
00:52:26,960 --> 00:52:38,320
equipment makers, you have their suppliers. Today, every hyperscaler is designing their own chips, and the scale of the hyperscalers is so large.
如今，每个超大规模云厂商都在自行设计芯片，而这些超大规模云厂商的规模之大，前所未有。

461
00:52:38,880 --> 00:52:51,760
Some of the model companies, for example, their demand is so high they could easily consume an entire fab’s worth of silicon on their own, or multiple fabs’ worth,
一些模型公司，比如，它们的需求如此之高，足以独自消耗一个晶圆厂的全部硅产能，甚至多个晶圆厂的产能，

462
00:52:53,040 --> 00:53:01,600
and they definitely have the capital to fund that. So in some sense, we are moving from a horizontal
而且它们确实有资金来支撑这种规模。因此在某种意义上，我们正从一个横向的世界，

463
00:53:01,600 --> 00:53:10,720
world again to a pseudo-vertical world. It’s not monolithically vertically integrated,
重新回到一种准垂直整合的世界。这并非完全一体化的垂直整合。

464
00:53:10,720 --> 00:53:21,760
but it’s kind of virtually vertically integrated. What is the nature of economies of scale in this business? If you look at how many
但它在某种程度上几乎是垂直整合的。这个行业的规模经济究竟如何？如果你看看台积电和英特

465
00:53:21,760 --> 00:53:28,000
wafers at the leading edge TSMC versus Intel is shipping, it’s upwards of 3X for TSMC.
尔在先进制程上出货的晶圆数量，台积电是英特尔的三倍以上。

466
00:53:28,960 --> 00:53:32,640
What kinds of advantages do you get at this point from economies of scale?
此时，规模经济能带来哪些优势？

467
00:53:32,640 --> 00:53:36,480
Or is it that once you reach a certain point, the economies of scale get weaker?
还是说，一旦达到某个临界点，规模经济就会减弱？

468
00:53:36,480 --> 00:53:49,680
That’s a very important question. This entire business just relies on economies of scale. If you look at all the advanced manufacturing
这是一个非常重要的问题。整个业务模式完全依赖于规模经济。如果你看看所有先进的制造企业，

469
00:53:49,680 --> 00:53:56,160
companies out there, in semiconductor foundries the scale economics is probably the purest.
半导体代工厂的规模经济可能是最纯粹的。

470
00:53:56,720 --> 00:54:00,720
The reason for that is the fixed cost is just so incredibly high.
原因是固定成本高得惊人。

471
00:54:02,160 --> 00:54:07,200
Once you build a fab, like 80% of your cost structure is fixed.
一旦建好了晶圆厂，大约80%的成本结构就固定了。

472
00:54:07,200 --> 00:54:16,160
What that means is every extra percentage of utilization is adding far more in revenue.
这意味着，每提高一个百分点的利用率，带来的收入增长要大得多。

473
00:54:16,160 --> 00:54:25,040
So it’s extremely important. We’ll fight for every extra percent of fab utilization, but also every extra percentage of fab output.
所以这至关重要。我们将为每多出1%的晶圆厂利用率而奋斗，也必将为每多出1%的晶圆厂产出而拼搏。

474
00:54:25,680 --> 00:54:30,960
Let’s say I’m making some wafers today and the yield is 60%.
假设我今天在生产晶圆，良率是60%。

475
00:54:30,960 --> 00:54:38,960
If I improve the process and the die yield becomes 65% or 70%, it’s not a linear advantage.
如果我优化了工艺，晶圆良率提升到65%或70%，带来的优势并不是线性的。

476
00:54:38,960 --> 00:54:41,760
I’m getting way more output for the given footprint.
我在相同的占地面积下，获得了远超以往的输出。

477
00:54:42,400 --> 00:54:45,920
So there’s always room to improve the economies of scale.
因此，规模经济始终有提升空间。

478
00:54:45,920 --> 00:54:53,440
That’s one of the reasons why you’re seeing so much consolidation in this business.
这也是为什么你看到这个行业出现如此多整合的原因之一。

479
00:54:53,440 --> 00:54:58,320
There are only three foundries left at the advanced node for that exact reason.
正因为如此，目前只剩下三家晶圆厂还在生产这一先进制程。

480
00:54:59,600 --> 00:55:04,000
Along the way, many companies just stopped manufacturing.
一路上，许多公司干脆停止了生产。

481
00:55:04,000 --> 00:55:13,840
I don’t know if you’ve seen… There’s a chart where 40 years ago there were like 20 foundries. Over time the number of foundries kept
我不知道你们有没有看到过一张图表，40年前全球有大约20家晶圆厂，但随着时间推移，晶圆厂数量不断减少，到现在只剩下三家。

482
00:55:13,840 --> 00:55:21,200
diminishing until today there are only three. What is then the concrete master plan to catch up to TSMC? Even though TSMC is ahead,
那么，面对台积电的领先地位，我们究竟有什么切实可行的追赶计划？尽管台积电目前遥遥领先，但其先进制程的产能已经严重供不应求，

483
00:55:21,200 --> 00:55:32,640
there’s so much overflow demand at their leading edge, where N3 is now majority AI accelerators and maybe majority Nvidia alone. There’s all this demand for CPUs, for other
目前N3制程的大部分产能都被AI加速芯片占据，甚至可以说，仅英伟达的需求就占了很大一部分。而与此同时，对CPU和其他产品的需求依然非常旺盛。

484
00:55:32,640 --> 00:55:39,360
things, which are also, by the way, needed for AI. There’s this humongous influx in demand for CPUs because of AI. How do you see this opportunity right now,
这些也是AI所需的。由于AI的兴起，对CPU的需求急剧增长。如今，英特尔落后了几年，

485
00:55:39,360 --> 00:55:44,080
where Intel’s a few years behind, while TSMC is just way oversaturated?
而台积电却已严重产能饱和，您如何看待这一机遇？

486
00:55:44,080 --> 00:55:52,560
Catching up to TSMC has multiple facets. The catch-up that we need to do is in scale.
追赶台积电涉及多个方面，我们需要追赶的主要是规模。

487
00:55:52,560 --> 00:56:07,360
Like you said, they’re just a much larger wafer manufacturer today than we are, and even bigger than what Samsung is. Catching up in scale will be a gradual process.
正如你所说，他们现在的晶圆制造规模比我们大得多，甚至超过了三星。在规模上追上将是一个渐进的过程。

488
00:56:08,880 --> 00:56:18,000
If you meant catching up in terms of just the technology, I think we’re already there.
如果你指的是在技术层面赶上的话，我认为我们已经做到了。

489
00:56:18,560 --> 00:56:27,600
We might be a few percent off on performance or power or area density.
我们的性能、功耗或面积密度可能有百分之几的偏差。

490
00:56:28,240 --> 00:56:38,160
But this sort of benchmarking, where one foundry is ahead or one foundry is behind,
但当一个代工厂落后或领先整整一代，

491
00:56:38,160 --> 00:56:45,200
makes sense when you’re a whole generation behind or two generations behind or ahead.
甚至两代时，这种对比才更有意义。

492
00:56:45,200 --> 00:56:57,413
That’s when it makes a meaningful difference in your die output or your die area or functionality like power. But if you’re within a few percent,
这时，它对芯片产出、芯片面积或功能（如功耗）才会产生实质性影响。但如果已经相差几个百分点，

493
00:56:59,520 --> 00:57:05,600
you’re splitting hairs at that point in time, because it’s not very unidimensional.
那在当下就只是在吹毛求疵，因为影响因素并非单一维度。

494
00:57:06,240 --> 00:57:11,040
It’s not just about density, it’s not just about raw performance.
这不仅关乎密度，也不仅关乎原始性能。

495
00:57:11,920 --> 00:57:17,200
Different applications require different vectors. You can be off by a few percent,
不同应用需要不同的向量。你可以有百分之几的误差，

496
00:57:17,200 --> 00:57:26,400
but you need to be on time. Your execution needs to be such that your yields are very high to support very large and very rapid ramps.
但必须准时。你的执行必须足够精准，才能实现极高的良率，以支持大规模且快速的产能提升。

497
00:57:27,120 --> 00:57:33,440
That actually is more important than catching up on a few percent in performance or area.
实际上，这比在性能或面积上追上几个百分点更重要。

498
00:57:33,440 --> 00:57:43,520
I was looking at some SemiAnalysis numbers that seem to suggest that in terms of transistor density, 14A — which will come out in 2028 — would be comparable to N2.
我看了下SemiAnalysis的数据，似乎表明在晶体管密度方面，2028年推出的14A工艺将与N2工艺相当。

499
00:57:43,520 --> 00:57:47,280
First of all, I would dispute your benchmarking.
首先，我得质疑一下你们的基准测试。

500
00:57:47,280 --> 00:57:56,880
But like I said, it’s important to be generally in the right ballpark and be very predictable.
但正如我所说，重要的是大致方向要对，而且要非常可预测。

501
00:57:56,880 --> 00:58:07,760
Also, it’s not unidimensional. We don’t build one type of transistor, let’s say 14A or 18A. You build a few different types of transistors.
而且，这并不是单一维度的。我们不会只制造一种晶体管，比如14A或18A。你需要制造几种不同类型的晶体管。

502
00:58:08,400 --> 00:58:13,520
There’s a low-leakage transistor, there’s a high-performance, high drive current transistor.
有一种低漏电晶体管，还有一种高性能、高驱动电流的晶体管。

503
00:58:13,520 --> 00:58:18,720
You’re building SRAMs. There’s a high-density SRAM, there’s a high-performance SRAM.
你在制造静态随机存取存储器。有高密度的 SRAM，也有高性能的 SRAM。

504
00:58:18,720 --> 00:58:24,640
There are many other functionalities like analog circuits, RF circuits.
还有许多其他功能，比如模拟电路、射频电路。

505
00:58:24,640 --> 00:58:29,520
It’s the sum of all of those that makes a technology competitive.
正是所有这些因素的总和，才使得一项技术具备竞争力。

506
00:58:29,520 --> 00:58:32,400
I guess that makes the CapEx you do right now even more important.
我想这使得你现在所做的资本开支更加重要了。

507
00:58:34,000 --> 00:58:38,000
TSMC is on track to do around $50 billion in CapEx this year.
台积电今年的资本开支预计将达到约500亿美元。

508
00:58:38,000 --> 00:58:46,800
You’re on track to do around $20 billion. If the tools you’re buying right now will determine who can service all this crazy AI demand that’s coming up, it makes the difference
你有望实现约200亿美元的营收。如果现在购买的工具将决定谁能满足即将到来的疯狂AI需求，那么当前资本开支的重要性就更加凸显，

509
00:58:46,800 --> 00:58:52,160
in CapEx right now even more important, or for the US versus other countries.
这甚至关系到美国与其他国家之间的竞争。

510
00:58:52,160 --> 00:59:05,680
Yes, very much. It’s a little bit of an unfair comparison between us and TSMC today, just because today they’re a foundry for basically everybody and we’re largely a foundry for Intel.
是的，非常有。今天把我们和台积电做比较其实不太公平，毕竟现在台积电基本上是为所有人提供代工服务，而我们主要还是为英特尔提供代工。

511
00:59:05,680 --> 00:59:11,200
We’ll become a foundry for others. In due course, our CapEx will also grow.
我们将成为他人的代工厂。届时，我们的资本开支也将随之增长。

512
00:59:11,200 --> 00:59:19,520
Intel started as a DRAM business in the late ’60s. You guys were producing DRAM, which you sold off.
英特尔上世纪60年代末起步时是一家动态随机存取存储器公司。你们当时生产DRAM，后来把这项业务卖掉了。

513
00:59:19,520 --> 00:59:22,160
A couple years ago, you sold off your flash business.
几年前，你把闪存业务卖掉了。

514
00:59:25,520 --> 00:59:35,680
Thoughts on that? For context for the audience, these are now multi-trillion-dollar industries because they’ve just exploded as a result of AI demand.
对此有什么看法？给观众一些背景：由于AI需求的爆发，这些产业如今已发展成规模达数万亿美元的行业。

515
00:59:36,960 --> 00:59:42,640
Clearly, in hindsight, probably not the smartest time to sell off a flash memory business.
显然，事后看来，出售闪存业务可能并不是一个明智的决定。

516
00:59:42,640 --> 00:59:45,440
There were good reasons why we did that when we did.
我们当时那样做是有充分理由的。

517
00:59:46,400 --> 01:00:00,560
Clearly, nobody could see this enormous demand. But today, the world will eat up as much compute
显然，没人能预见这种巨大的需求。但如今，全世界能生产的算力

518
01:00:00,560 --> 01:00:06,000
as can possibly be made, and it’ll eat up as much memory as can possibly be made.
将被全部消耗殆尽，能制造出的内存也将被全部用完。

519
01:00:06,000 --> 01:00:12,960
I would argue that the supply crunch is larger for memory today than it is for compute.
我认为，当前内存的供应短缺比计算资源更为严重。

520
01:00:12,960 --> 01:00:16,560
Can I summarize what seems to me the takeaways from your projections?
我可以总结一下您预测中在我看来的要点吗？

521
01:00:16,560 --> 01:00:17,760
You can tell me if that’s correct.
你可以告诉我这是否正确。

522
01:00:17,760 --> 01:00:27,200
That marginal production can increase actually very fast — on the scale of 12 months — but the amount of increase up till the end of the decade is extremely limited.
边际产能的提升实际上可以非常迅速——在12个月的规模内——但到本十年末，其增长幅度将极为有限。

523
01:00:27,200 --> 01:00:33,040
Production could be higher next year than what people are forecasting, but production in 2030 is not going to be significantly higher than what people are forecasting.
明年的产量可能会高于人们的预测，但到2030年，产量不会比当前预测值有显著提升。

524
01:00:33,040 --> 01:00:36,320
Unless the entire supply chain ramps up production very quickly.
除非整个供应链能迅速提升产能。

525
01:00:36,320 --> 01:00:42,560
We’re already in the middle of ’26, so there’s not that many years until the end of the decade.
我们已经进入2026年中，距离本十年结束已没几年了。

526
01:00:42,560 --> 01:00:52,240
Given the lead times… Let’s say you place an enormous order for tools today, and you’re looking at 18 months to get those tools.
考虑到交期……假如你今天下了一个巨大的设备订单，最快也要18个月才能拿到这些设备。

527
01:00:52,240 --> 01:01:02,160
You’re already into basically ’28. From that point on, if you have to build a new shell, build a new fab, you’re basically looking at the end of the decade.
你已经快进入2028年了。从那时起，如果你需要新建一个外壳或一个新的晶圆厂，基本上要到本世纪末才能完成。

528
01:01:02,160 --> 01:01:07,760
I think it’ll be interesting to discuss the partnership you guys have with Elon on Terafab.
我觉得讨论你们与埃隆在Terafab项目上的合作会很有趣。

529
01:01:07,760 --> 01:01:19,840
What’s going on there? Well, Elon’s publicly talked about his desire to build a fab. We either build the Terafab or
那里发生了什么？埃隆公开表示过他想建一座晶圆厂。我们要么建 Terafab，

530
01:01:19,840 --> 01:01:25,600
we don’t have the chips. And we need the chips, so we’re going to build the Terafab. Sort of the same thinking.
要么就造不出芯片。而我们确实需要芯片，所以必须建 Terafab。思路差不多就是这样。

531
01:01:26,320 --> 01:01:34,800
He’s seeing just an enormous increase in demand for silicon, for compute and for memory.
他看到对硅、计算能力和内存的需求急剧增长。

532
01:01:34,800 --> 01:01:40,320
I think he came to the conclusion that his own businesses are going to require a lot of silicon.
我认为他得出的结论是，自己的企业将需要大量硅。

533
01:01:42,480 --> 01:01:46,160
He thought maybe he should just do it all on his own.
他心想，也许他应该自己全部搞定。

534
01:01:46,160 --> 01:01:59,440
But unlike building a car, for example, this is such an intricate and complicated process that we thought we could work together to help accelerate his bringing up his fab.
但与造车这类项目不同，这个过程极其复杂精密，所以我们认为可以携手合作，加快他建设晶圆厂的进程。

535
01:01:59,440 --> 01:02:09,360
But because of the bottleneck at the tool level, it seems like the net amount of silicon in the world won’t increase. It’s just that maybe if somebody were to start
但由于设备层面的瓶颈，全球硅产量似乎不会增加。也许只有当有人新建晶圆厂时，

536
01:02:09,360 --> 01:02:18,160
a new fab, they would get access to guaranteed supply in a way that they wouldn’t have if they’re working with another person’s foundry. It’s not just tools.
才能获得保障的供应，而如果依赖别人的代工厂，则无法做到这一点。这不仅仅是设备的问题。

537
01:02:18,800 --> 01:02:24,000
A fab does require a lot of power, a lot of water, a lot of consumables.
晶圆厂确实需要大量电力、大量水以及大量耗材。

538
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:34,400
There are many other input variables as well. But those can be scaled in the way that tools cannot. Some more than others, but yes.
还有许多其他输入变量。但这些变量的扩展方式是工具无法实现的，虽然有些比其他变量更难扩展，但确实如此。

539
01:02:34,400 --> 01:02:42,640
In one sense, yes, we need a lot more equipment. But it is true, we also just need a lot more raw
从某种意义上说，是的，我们需要更多的设备。但事实是，我们同样需要更多

540
01:02:42,640 --> 01:02:48,480
fab space, raw clean room capacity. If you think of a foundry like Intel,
的原始晶圆厂空间和原始洁净室产能。如果你想想英特尔这样的代工厂，

541
01:02:48,480 --> 01:02:59,280
which has to build these expensive facilities and buy expensive tools, I wonder what you think the economics of that fundamentally could look like, whether it could get more economical,
它必须建造这些昂贵的设施并购买昂贵的设备，我不禁好奇，这种模式的经济性从根本上可能是什么样子的，它是否有可能变得更经济？

542
01:02:59,280 --> 01:03:03,120
whether it could scale faster. Like I described earlier,
它能否更快地实现规模化。正如我之前所描述的，

543
01:03:03,120 --> 01:03:19,520
this is an extremely capital-heavy business. If you just plot how much it costs to build a fab over the last 20, 30, 40 years, I think it’s doubling every 4-5 years.
这是一项资本极度密集的业务。如果你画出过去20年、30年、40年建设晶圆厂的成本变化，我认为成本每4到5年就会翻一番。

544
01:03:19,520 --> 01:03:31,040
Even inside the fab, if you look at the cost to do lithography, which is the most expensive module — just patterning — that itself is doubling at more than that rate.
即使在晶圆厂内部，如果你看看光刻的成本，它是最昂贵的模块——仅是图案化这一项——其成本增长速度就超过了这个速率。

545
01:03:31,680 --> 01:03:40,560
Today, a High-NA EUV scanner and all its associated equipment costs like $400 million.
如今，一台高数值孔径 EUV 光刻机及其所有相关设备的造价约为 4 亿美元。

546
01:03:41,280 --> 01:03:44,560
So what does that mean? If that rate were to continue,
那意味着什么？如果这个速度持续下去，

547
01:03:44,560 --> 01:03:56,640
if you just draw the line, does that mean four years from now the cost of patterning is going to be like $800 million? It doesn’t really make sense.
你只要画条直线，是不是意味着四年之后光刻成本会达到8亿美元？这显然说不通。

548
01:03:58,160 --> 01:04:14,160
A very interesting thought exercise is to ask, are there things that could be just very radically different that we could do today that we perhaps couldn’t do 20 years ago?
一个非常有趣的思想实验是，思考一下我们今天是否能做些与过去截然不同的事情，而这些事情在20年前我们可能根本无法实现。

549
01:04:14,160 --> 01:04:19,440
This is a very risk-averse industry for very good reason, for exactly that reason.
这是一个非常谨慎的行业，原因非常充分，正是出于这个原因。

550
01:04:19,440 --> 01:04:25,200
You put in tens of billions of dollars, and you really don’t want to take any risks.
你投入了数百亿美元，真的不愿承担任何风险。

551
01:04:25,200 --> 01:04:29,600
You want to keep doing things the way they’ve been done for literally decades.
你却还想继续用几十年来一直沿用的方式做事。

552
01:04:29,600 --> 01:04:32,960
This is not just true of semiconductors, but everything really.
这不仅适用于半导体，实际上对所有事物都成立。

553
01:04:33,760 --> 01:04:40,800
If you’ve just kept on doing that for decade upon decade, you just become indoctrinated.
如果你几十年如一日地一直这么做，就会不知不觉被洗脑。

554
01:04:42,080 --> 01:04:53,680
There are engineers who probably work at Intel who are new, who come in and they’re taught, “This is how the fab works, this is the process flow.” They just take that as gospel.
有些工程师可能刚加入英特尔，他们一进来就会被教导：“晶圆厂是这样运作的，工艺流程是这样的。”他们就把这些当作金科玉律。

555
01:04:53,680 --> 01:05:02,480
That’s just how it is done. But is that really the best way to build a chip today? It’s a really valid question.
这就是目前的做法。但这真的是当今构建芯片的最佳方式吗？这是一个非常合理的问题。

556
01:05:02,480 --> 01:05:13,360
I’m very curious what you’re hinting at, because it could be anything from figuring out ways to use fewer litho passes and economize on this expensive component, to maybe we need to
我很好奇你暗示的是什么，因为这可能涉及减少光刻步骤、节省这个昂贵组件的使用，也可能是我们需要重新思考晶体管的设计。

557
01:05:13,360 --> 01:05:25,040
rethink the transistor. What is the level of— All of the above. Transistor architecture is evolving.
所有这些都有可能。晶体管架构正在不断演进。

558
01:05:25,840 --> 01:05:33,200
Just the silicon transistor has continued to evolve over 30, 40 years, and that will continue.
硅晶体管在过去三四十年间持续演进，这一趋势还将继续。

559
01:05:33,200 --> 01:05:44,320
You’ve seen the evolution from the planar transistor to FinFETs to now gate-all-around transistors. There’ll be more. That roadmap exists, it’s pretty
你已经看到晶体管从平面结构演进到鳍式场效应晶体管，再到如今的环绕栅极晶体管。未来还会有更多进展。

560
01:05:44,320 --> 01:05:49,440
well defined, with a lot of people working on it. But there are things like…
这条技术路线已经明确，有很多人正在为此努力。但有些问题值得思考：

561
01:05:51,120 --> 01:06:04,000
Do you really need a clean room today? Do you need the infrastructure to be as sophisticated, as complex as it is today? Surely one can run an experiment where you
今天真的需要洁净室吗？所需的基础设施是否必须像现在这样复杂？显然，完全可以开展一些实验来验证这一点。

562
01:06:05,520 --> 01:06:11,360
have people eating hamburgers and smoking cigars in the research fab and see what happens to yield.
让一些人在研发晶圆厂里吃汉堡、抽雪茄，看看良率会变成什么样。

563
01:06:12,880 --> 01:06:25,360
That would be a great experiment to run, but no incumbent — Intel or TSMC or Samsung — would want to willingly do that. That’s what I meant by this.
这会是一个很棒的实验，但没有一家现有企业——无论是英特尔、台积电还是三星——会愿意主动去这么做。我刚才说的就是这个意思。

564
01:06:26,160 --> 01:06:35,840
It’s a very risk-averse business, for good reason. Every one day lost in productivity is… I don’t know how many dollars. But you save so much money in
这是一门非常谨慎的生意，原因很充分。每天生产效率的损失……我也不知道具体是多少美元。

565
01:06:35,840 --> 01:06:41,360
not having to build clean rooms in the future. But it’s really the risk, the fear of the unknown.
但未来不用再建洁净室，能省下大量资金。真正关键的是风险，对未知的恐惧。

566
01:06:41,360 --> 01:06:49,600
Nobody is going to willingly take that risk. That’s why I think sometimes it’s good to just
没有人会愿意主动承担这种风险。所以我认为，有时候从一张白纸开始，

567
01:06:49,600 --> 01:06:59,440
start with a clean slate and rethink everything. The same thing applies to chip design, by the way.
重新思考一切，反而是件好事。顺便说一句，芯片设计也是如此。

568
01:06:59,440 --> 01:07:06,720
Many of the ways we do CPU design were probably locked in 30 years ago.
我们进行CPU设计的许多方式，可能早在30年前就已经定型了。

569
01:07:06,720 --> 01:07:11,360
Does that mean it’s smart to still do it the same way?
这意味着仍以相同方式行事是明智的吗？

570
01:07:11,360 --> 01:07:25,120
Probably not. But this is a very complex problem. In chip design, I guess the capacity to take
可能不会。但这个问题非常复杂。在芯片设计中，我猜承担风险的能力可能更高一些，

571
01:07:25,120 --> 01:07:29,840
risk is maybe higher. This one, less so.
而这个则没那么高。

572
01:07:29,840 --> 01:07:35,360
But given where the economics is today, I think it’s a very valid question to ask.
但考虑到当前的经济形势，我认为这是一个非常合理的问题。

573
01:07:36,720 --> 01:07:47,760
Maybe there’s a smarter way to do this. To close out, maybe we’ll start off at the same question we began the interview with, which is, how does Intel respond to this crazy AI
也许有更好的方法来应对这个问题。最后，我们不妨回到采访开始时提出的问题：英特尔将如何应对这股汹涌而至、

574
01:07:47,760 --> 01:07:58,560
demand that’s incoming and has already arrived? It sounds like what Intel ought to do — if the ultimate bottleneck is that you guys already have these foundries, you already have the
已然到来的AI需求？听起来，英特尔真正该做的——如果最终的瓶颈在于你们已经拥有晶圆厂，

575
01:07:58,560 --> 01:08:10,480
ability to expand these foundries, but what you will eventually lack is the tools from upstream in the supply chain — is to just raise as much capital as possible right now.
也具备扩展这些晶圆厂的能力，但未来可能缺乏供应链上游的工具——就是现在就尽可能多地筹集资金。

576
01:08:10,480 --> 01:08:21,120
The same way that Nvidia has locked down memory and other components for reportedly hundreds of billions of dollars worth of commitments that it’s made, Intel should maybe do the same thing:
就像英伟达已为数百亿美元的承诺锁定内存及其他组件一样，英特尔或许也该采取类似做法：做出这些承诺，

577
01:08:21,120 --> 01:08:32,720
make all these commitments so that over the coming years, the US is in good position to have the bulk of semiconductor manufacturing at the most advanced levels happening here.
以便在未来几年内，美国能处于有利地位，使最先进制程的半导体制造大部分发生在这里。

578
01:08:32,720 --> 01:08:35,600
You’re suggesting we should be buying a lot more tools, right?
你是说我们应该多买些工具，对吧？

579
01:08:35,600 --> 01:08:42,640
Yeah, because it sounds like otherwise in a couple years you’d just be in a position where you’re like, “Oh man, we could be making trillions of dollars right now,
是啊，因为听起来再过几年，你就会发现自己处于这种境地：“天哪，我们现在本可以赚取万亿 dollars，

580
01:08:42,640 --> 01:08:47,840
but all the tools are in Taiwan.” Yes, that’s a very valid question.
但所有工具都在台湾。” 是的，这是个非常合理的问题。

581
01:08:47,840 --> 01:08:52,960
Had we known what we know today, we would have invested more like a year ago.
如果我们今天能预知到这些，一年前就会投入更多。

582
01:08:52,960 --> 01:08:57,600
Knowing what we know now, I think we’ll be investing more.
了解了我们现在所知道的情况后，我认为我们会加大投资。
